\textbf{Pull-mode v.s. Push-mode.} 
\autoref{fig:eva:push-pull} illustrates the comparison between pull-mode and push-mode, demonstrating that pull-mode leads to an average speedup of 25.5\% over push-mode. This improvement primarily stems from reduced waiting times at the decode worker, which are recorded as the prefill time. As the QPS increases, resource idling results in an additional $1.6\times$ increase in queuing time for the KV cache on the decode worker. Additionally, reserving KV cache on the decode worker in push-mode limits the number of concurrent ongoing requests. This constraint results in fewer interruptions between the generations of two consecutive tokens of the same request, thereby reducing the TBT by 5.6\% and 14.4\% across the datasets, respectively. However, despite these improvements in TBT, the total latency still increases in push-mode, primarily because waiting for KV cache becomes a significant bottleneck when the QPS is high.

