\subsection{Numerical Validation for Satellite Docking Scenario}
This subsection validates the coil geometry learning by two satellite docking simulations. The position and attitude controllers are PD controllers designed using a coefficient diagram method \cite{manabe1998coefficient} and executed at 0.1-second intervals. At each time, we update the target values for the controller based on the final target values using third-order splines to suppress control current fluctuations. Thanks to the generated target value profile, we have successfully constrained the current within acceptable limits in simulation, avoiding exceeding the set upper limit of $\pm 10 A$. Table~\ref{satellite_specification_and_orbit} summarizes the satellite specifications and orbit altitudes. Note that we observed the conservation of angular momentum for all simulations, affirming the validity of our simulation. The initial values and final target values are summarized in Table~\ref{initila_and_final_target_values}. As stated in the assumptions, the target satellite applies the predetermined current in Eq.~(\ref{decentralized_constant_current}), and the chaser satellite calculates its current in the decentralized manner in Eq.~(\ref{decentralized_current_allocation}). We note that the actual magnetic field calculation is conducted by the near-field model for all numerical calculations. 

We first compare the docking results based on the far-field approximation model in Eq.~(\ref{far_field_model}) and the exact model in Eq.~(\ref{exact_magnetic_field}) to highlight the effectiveness of the near-field model. Figure~\ref {3D_docking_control_results} presents an overview of the three-dimensional simulation results, and the target satellite values are depicted in gray, and the chaser satellite in red. Figure~\ref {3D_docking_control_results} (a,b) are the control results using the far-field model-based calculation (see the details of the calculation based on the far-field approximation in Appendix~\ref{appendix_far_field_approximation}). Figure~\ref{Docking_distance_force_angular_torque} shows the time evolution of the position and attitude states and electromagnetic control inputs. Dashed lines represent results based on the far-field model, while solid lines represent those based on the near-field model. We can observe that significant control input errors occur between the two models at approximately 250 seconds. This extensive control effort leads to a collision as shown in Fig.~\ref {3D_docking_control_results} (b). Conversely, Fig.~\ref{3D_docking_control_results} (c) shows that employing the near-field Model enables both relative distance and absolute attitude control through magnetic field interaction, even at proximity for randomly generated 40 initial states. Here, the 1st satellite accomplishes attitude control using reaction wheels, based on Eq.~(\ref{reaction_wheel}), to converge its attitude to the target and mitigate electromagnetic torque disturbances. It is worth noting 2nd satellite achieves attitude control only by using electromagnetic torque without reaction wheels that reaffirms the effectiveness of employing electromagnetic torque for attitude control  \cite{takahashi2020time,takahashi2021simultaneous,takahashi2024neural,ayyad2019optimal,abbasi2022decentralized}.%Furthermore, from the results of the control currents shown in Figure