\subsection{Orbital and Attitude Motion of an Earth Orbiting Satellite}
This subsection introduces the relative motion and attitude dynamics using RW. The relative motion of two satellites orbiting in a circular orbit is denoted in the orbital coordinate system $\{o\}$ frame:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\ddot{x}+2 \omega_o \dot{y}-3 \omega_o^2 x  =\frac{F_x}{m_c} \\
&\ddot{y}-2 \omega_o \dot{x} =\frac{F_y}{m_c}, \quad\ddot{z}+\omega_o^2 z =\frac{F_z}{m_c}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $m_c\in\mathbb{R}$ represents the mass of the chaser satellite, $\omega_o\in\mathbb{R}$ denotes the orbital frequency given by $\omega_0=$ $\sqrt{\mu_g / R_o^3}\in\mathbb{R}$ with the geocentric gravitational constant $\mu_g\in\mathbb{R}$ and the satellite's orbital radius $R_o\in\mathbb{R}$. Note that this study mainly focuses on the actuator level problem, and then, the inter-satellite distance is set to a relatively small value to neglect the dynamics effect. Finally, the following equation shows the attitude dynamics for a satellite equipped with a reaction wheel (RW):
\begin{equation}
    \label{reaction_wheel}
%\begin{aligned}
J \cdot \dot\omega+\omega \times\left(J \cdot \omega+h_{R W}\right) =-\tau_{R W}+\tau_d,\ \dot{h}_{R W}=\tau_{R W}
%\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $J\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times 3}$ is the moment of inertia, $\omega\in\mathbb{R}^{3}$ is the aircraft angular velocity, $h_{R W}\in\mathbb{R}^3$ and $\tau_{R W}$ are the angular momentum of RW and torque, and $\tau_d\in\mathbb{R}^3$ is the disturbance torque.
\subsection{``Far-field'' Approximation Model}
\label{appendix_far_field_approximation}
``Far-field'' model \cite{schweighart2006electromagnetic} provides a computationally friendly approximated magnetic field. We define the relative vector $s_{ij}\in\mathbb{R}^3$ between the geometric center of two coils, and the position vector $R_{i}\in\mathbb{R}$ from the geometric center into ${\mathrm{d}l}_i$ such that $r_{ij}=s_{ij}-R_{i}$. We assume the size of the coil loop is much smaller than the relative distance, i.e., $|R_{i}|\ll|s_{ij}|$. Then, we get
$$
|R_{i}|\ll|s_{ij}| \Rightarrow \frac{1}{|r|}\approx \frac{1}{|s_{ij}|}+\frac{s_{ij}\cdot R_{i}}{|s_{ij}|^3}
$$
This approximation convert the multi-layer coil derives dipole model, i.e., $j$th magnetic moment $\mu_{j}(t)\in\mathbb{R}^3$ [Am$^2$] at time $t$ and its magnetic field ${B}_k\left({\mu}_k, {r}_{j k}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^3$ [T] are 
\begin{equation}
\label{far_field_model}
%\mu_{j}(t)=N_t c(t) A\bm{n}
%\left(1+\frac{\left(\mu_r-1\right)}{\left(1-N_d+\mu_r N_d\right)}\right)
B_k({\mu}_k, {r}_{j k})=\frac{\mu_0}{4 \pi d_{jk}^3}\left(3M_k\mathsf{e}_r -{\mu}_k\right)
\end{equation}
where $\mu_{k}$ is in Eq.~(\ref{dipole_moment}), $M_k=\bm{\mu}_k \cdot \mathsf{e}_r$, $d_{jk}=\left\|\bm{r}_{j k}\right\|$, and $\mathsf{e}_r={\bm{r}_{j k}}/{d_{jk}}$. This model is accurate if the relative distance $s_{ij}$ exceeds around twice the diameter of the coil, i.e., $2R_{i}$ \cite{sakaguchi2005micro}. This also simplifies the magnetic field interaction model, the electromagnetic force and torque exerted on the $j$-th agent by the $k$-th one as $\bm{f}_{j\leftarrow k}\left(\bm{\mu}_{j,k}, \bm{r}_{j k}\right)=\nabla({\mu}_j \cdot {B}_k)$ and $\bm{\tau}_{j\leftarrow k}\left(\bm{\mu}_{j,k}, \bm{r}_{j k}\right)=\bm{\mu}_j \times B_k$ where
\begin{equation}
\label{far_field_magnetic_interaction} 
%\begin{aligned}
\bm{f}_{j\leftarrow k}=\frac{3 \mu_0}{4 \pi d^4}\left(({\bm{\mu}_k \cdot \bm{\mu}_j}-5 M_k M_j)\mathsf{e}_r+M_k \bm{\mu}_j+M_j\bm{\mu}_k\right).
%\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
