In addition, we present an \textbf{adaptive contrastive loss} to handle the varying utility of positive-negative signals (\textbf{Gap 3}). Unlike existing methods that assign the same weight to all positive-negative signals~\cite{Xie@ICDE2022, Du@CIKM2022}, our proposed adaptive contrastive loss evaluates the contribution of each signal on model performance using a simple yet effective neural network. Accordingly, this adaptive approach automatically highlights informative signals and diminishes uninformative ones during training, thereby facilitating effective self-supervised learning. Ultimately, we integrate user intent prediction and self-supervised tasks to enhance \baby. 
