\section{Hardness of $k$-IP Problem} 

In this section, we prove the NP-completeness of the $k$-IP problem on directed acyclic graphs when $k \ge 4$. Trivially, the $k$-IP problem is in NP. We use a similar reduction as in the NP-hardness proof of the IP problem. In fact, we show that the $k$-IP problem is NP-hard even when all the edge weights are equal to $1$. If the edge weights are equal to $1$, the weight of all the paths of length $k$ is the same and equal to $\log{((k+1)!)}$. This implies that we only need to prove the NP-hardness of the following decision problem.

\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=white, colframe=teal, sharp corners=all, boxrule=0.25mm, rounded corners=all, width=\textwidth]
\textbf{Edge-Disjoint $k$-Paths Problem (for a fixed integer $k \ge 4$)}:\\
\textbf{Input:} A directed acyclic graph $G=(V,E)$ and a positive integer $t$. \\
\textbf{Question:} Are there $t$ edge-disjoint $k$-paths in $G$?
\end{tcolorbox}

\begin{thm} \label{thm:kip}
    The $3$-SAT problem reduces polynomially to edge-disjoint $k$-paths problem.
\end{thm}

\begin{proof}
    First, we prove the statement for $k = 4$. Let the Boolean formula $f = C_1 \land C_2 \land C_3 \land \cdots \land C_m$  over the variables $x_1, x_2, x_3, \cdots, x_n$ be an instance of $3$-SAT problem. We assume that no clause of $f$ contains both positive and negative literal of same variable. Consider the same graph $G_f$ as in the proof of \cref{thm:ip} (Refer to \cref{fig:gadgets}).

    Recall that graph $G_f$ has $O(nm)$ vertices. The instance for the edge-disjoint $4$-paths problem is $G_f$ with parameter $t = m + \sum_{i=1}^{n} \ell_i$. To finish the argument we only need to prove the following claim.

    \begin{clm}
    The formula $f$ is satisfiable if and only of $G_f$ contains at least $m + \sum_{i=1}^{n} \ell_i$ edge-disjoint $4$-paths.
    \end{clm}

    \begin{proof}
        Let $f$ be satisfiable. We find a set $\mathcal{P}'$ of $m + \sum_{i=1}^{n} \ell_i$ edge-disjoint $4$-paths in $G_f$. For each variable $x_i$, if $x_i$ is $\mathrm{TRUE}$, then we consider $\ell_i$ paths of length $4$ from $s_i$ to $t_i$ going through vertices labeled $\overline{y}_{i,\alpha_j}$ in $\mathcal{P}'$, for each $j \in \{1, 2, \cdots, \ell_i\}$; otherwise, we consider $\ell_i$ paths of length $4$ from $s_i$ to $t_i$ going through vertices labeled ${y}_{i,\alpha_j}$ in $\mathcal{P}'$.

        Moreover, for each clause there is at least one literal with a $\mathrm{TRUE}$ assignment. Let $x_{i}$ be a $\mathrm{TRUE}$ literal in $C_{\alpha_j}$. If $x_{i}$ appears as a positive literal in $C_{\alpha_j}$, then none of the paths from $s_i$ to $t_i$ pass through the edge $({y}_{i, \alpha_{j}}, u_{i,\alpha_{j+1}})$. Therefore, we consider the directed path of length $4$ form $z_{\alpha_j, 0}$ to $u_{i,\alpha_{j+1}}$ in $\mathcal{P}'$. Otherwise, $x_{i}$ appears as a negative literal in $C_{\alpha_j}$. That is, none of the paths from $s_i$ to $t_i$ pass through the edge $(\overline{y}_{i, \alpha_{j}}, u_{i,\alpha_{j}})$, so we consider the directed path of length $4$ from $z_{0, \alpha_j}$ to $u_{i,\alpha_{j}}$ in $\mathcal{P}'$. Clearly, we have included exactly $m + \sum_{i=1}^{n} \ell_i$ edge-disjoint $4$-paths in $\mathcal{P}'$.

        For the other direction of the proof, assume we have a set $\mathcal{P}$ of edge-disjoint $4$-paths of size at least $m + \sum_{i=1}^{n} \ell_i$. Note that each $4$-path in $\mathcal{P}$ starts in a vertex $s_i \in V(G_i)$ for some variable gadget $G_i$, or it starts in exactly one of the vertices in the set $\{z_{j,0},z_{j,1}\} \subset V(H_j)$ for some clause gadget $H_j$. So the total number of edge-disjoint paths of length $4$ is at most $m + \sum_{i=1}^n \ell_i$. Therefore, for each $i \in \{1,\cdots, n\}$, there are exactly $\ell_i$ $4$-paths starting at $s_i$, and for each $j \in \{1,\cdots,m\}$, there is a path $\Pi_j \in \mathcal{P}$ that starts at $z_{j,0}$.

        Observe that since $\Pi_j$ has length $4$, in the corresponding variable gadget $G_{\beta_j}$, it passes through either $y_{\beta_j, j}$ or $\overline{y}_{\beta_j, j}$. If $\Pi_j$ passes through the $y_{\beta_j, j}$, then we assign $\mathrm{TRUE}$ to the variable $x_{\beta_j}$ (in this case, the $\ell_i$ paths in $G_{\beta_j}$ are passing though the vertices labeled $\overline{y}$). Otherwise, if $\Pi_j$ passes through the $\overline{y}_{\beta_j, j}$, then we assign $\mathrm{FALSE}$ to the variable $x_{\beta_j}$ (in this case, the $\ell_i$ paths in $G_{\beta_j}$ are passing through the vertices labeled ${y}$).
        By the fact that the edges of each clause gadget are contained in some $4$-path, we are ensured that this assignment is satisfying for $f$. Note that some of the variables are not assigned a value; for those, we choose an arbitrary value.
    \end{proof}

    This finishes the proof of NP-hardness for the $k$-IP problem when $k = 4$. For $k > 4$, the proof arguments are exactly the same, except we subdivide each edge of the form $(s_i, v_{i,\alpha_j})$ and $(z_{j,0}, z_{j,1})$ in $G_f$ exactly $k-4$ times, for each $1 \le i \le n$ and $1 \le j \le m$.
\end{proof}

