\section{Conclusion}
% In this paper, we focused on data-efficiently utilizing self-supervised signals in the repository to improve the performance of existing models in various granularity code search scenarios.
% Initially, we constructed a code search dataset called MGCodeSearchNet using data from repositories. This dataset pairs natural language comments with code snippets at various levels of granularity.
% Subsequently, we introduced a novel multi-granularity code search framework, named MGS$^{3}$, which enhances code representation by modeling the hierarchical relationships between code snippets of different granularities. Additionally, MGS$^{3}$ strengthens the contrastive learning process by constructing positive and negative samples from code snippets of varying granularities. Experiments conducted on multi-granularity code search benchmarks have demonstrated MGS$^{3}$'s exceptional representation capabilities when dealing with code snippets of different granularities. Further analytical experiments have proven that our method has improved the interpretability of existing pre-trained models in code search tasks. In future work, we plan to expand the MGCodeSearchNet dataset by utilizing a wide range of online repositories, and we aim to pre-train MGS$^{3}$ on a larger corpus of source code to further enhance its capabilities and generalizability in code search.
In this paper, we aimed to efficiently leverage self-supervised signals from repositories to enhance model performance across various code search scenarios. We created a dataset, MGCodeSearchNet, which pairs natural language comments with code snippets at different granularities.
We then introduced MGS$^{3}$, a novel multi-granularity code search framework that improves code representation by modeling the hierarchical relationships between code snippets. MGS$^{3}$ also enhances contrastive learning by constructing positive and negative samples from code snippets of varying granularities.
Our experiments on multi-granularity code search benchmarks demonstrated MGS$^{3}$’s superior representation capabilities. Additionally, analytical experiments showed that our method improves the interpretability of pre-trained models in code search tasks.
% In future work, we plan to expand the MGCodeSearchNet dataset using a wider range of online repositories and pre-train MGS$^{3}$ on a larger corpus of source code to further enhance its performance and generalizability in code search.
In future work, we plan to expand the MGCodeSearchNet dataset using a wider range of online repositories and to attempt to combine MGS$^{3}$ with retrieval-augmented generation techniques to achieve better code generation performance.

