\section{Extra of Experiment}



\subsection{Results of the Fine-tuning Experiment.}
We present the results of the fine-tuning in~\autoref{tab:extraExperi}. By observing the experimental results on the CodeSearchNet dataset across various programming languages, it can be seen that by applying MGS$^3$ method, significant improvements can be achieved in all programming languages, which demonstrates the effectiveness of the MGS$^3$.

\subsection{Ablation Study}
\label{sec:ablation}
We attempted to present the complete ablation study experiment in~\autoref{tab:ablation}. We can observe that different granular data sets exhibit relatively similar changes for different ablation experiment settings. In the block-level experimental results, we observed a significant performance drop in the SO-DS benchmark after deleting $\mathcal{L}_s$. We speculate that this is because the benchmark comes from StackOverflow, and during the collection process, some statement-level code snippets were mixed in.

\subsection{Parameter Sensitivity Experiments}
\label{sec:paraSensty}
For the weights $\alpha$ and $\beta$ in the loss functions of different granularities, we conducted parameter sensitivity experiments using the CodeBERT model on Adv, StaQC, and CoNaLa. Table 6 shows the performance of $\alpha$ and $\beta$ at 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, and 1. When adjusting one of the parameters, we set the other to 0 to facilitate the observation of results. 

% We observed that changing the parameter $\alpha$ resulted in little change in the final model performance, but adjusting the $\beta$ parameter actually led to a significant decline in function-level and block-level retrieval performance. We speculate that this is because block-level code snippets, due to their moderate granularity and high quality, do not actually affect other granular content. In contrast, statement-level snippets contain less information and, due to their lower granularity, tend to generate more noise. To balance the model's performance across different granularity retrieval scenarios, we ultimately set $\alpha$ to 1 and $\beta$ to 0.6.


