\section{Introduction}
Cued Speech (CS) is an advanced visual phonetic encoding system that utilizes specific hand shapes and positions to enhance lip reading, providing an accurate visual representation of all phonemes in spoken language \cite{cornett1967cued,liu2019pilot,leybaert2010cued}. 
CS maintains a high level of consistency with spoken language in terms of phonemes and speech patterns, enabling hearing-impaired individuals to better integrate into speech-dominant social and educational environments \cite{cornett1967cued,leybaert2010cued,leybaert201019}. 
% As a crucial communication tool, CS has gained increasing global attention and has been adapted for use with more than sixty spoken languages. 
In Mandarin Chinese, CS employs 8 hand shapes and 5 positions to encode consonants and vowels, addressing challenges such as the phonemes with similar lip shapes \cite{liu2019pilot}, as illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:overview}(a). 



\textbf{CS V}ideo-to-\textbf{S}peech generation (\textbf{CSV2S}) task aims to convert the
visual expressions (CS videos) of hearing-impaired individuals into comprehensible speech signals.
However, directly constructing an end-to-end CSV2S model faces the following challenges: 
 \textbf{First, the complex semantic correlation} between CS video and semantic content requires precise mapping from visual cues (lip reading and hand coding) to speech, while limited CS dataset scale further constrains model capacity.
\textbf{Second, fine-grained visual spatiotemporal modeling} is crucial to resolve the natural asynchrony where hand gestures initiate earlier than corresponding lip movements \textit{i.e.,} hand-preceding phenomenon \cite{liu2020re}.
\textbf{Third, personalized adaptation} must address dual variations: (1) inter-cuer differences in CS expression, and (2) acoustic diversity in cuer identity characteristics. \textbf{To the best of our knowledge, no prior work has addressed CSV2S task.}

Existing research primarily focuses on \textbf{CS R}ecognition (\textbf{CSR}) that converts CS videos into phoneme-level text\cite{liu2020re,liu2024computation,liu2018visual,liu2019automatic}, neglecting the critical need for natural speech generation. This gap severely limits real-time communication between hearing-impaired and normal-hearing individuals, particularly in educational and social contexts. For example, in social gatherings, normal-hearing individuals may need to quickly and accurately understand and respond to questions or information shared by their hearing-impaired friends. However, existing CSR methods can only provide text output, which is insufficient to support natural and smooth interactions. Moreover, lip-reading video-to-speech synthesis methods like LipVoicer \cite{yemini2023lipvoicer} leverage lip motions alone, and they fail to model the complementary hand coding in CS. 
Thus, the primary motivation of this study is \textbf{to build the first CSV2S system that directly decoding Chinese CS video into speech}, as shown in Figure \ref{fig:overview}(b).

 Based on the existing CSR methods, a straightforward approach to build CSV2S is to combine CSR with a Text-to-Speech (TTS) model, as shown in Figure \ref{fig:motivation}(a). However, this combined architecture has fundamental drawbacks. Firstly, the intermediate CS text serve as a medium between visual and speech, inheriting recognition errors from the CSR module, leading to inaccurate speech content. Secondly, the CSR stage only extracts semantic text but loses fine-grained temporal information from video, resulting in generated speech lacking temporal alignment. 



 % due to the scarcity of CS data, and the complex fine-grained spatiotemporal mapping relationship between CS visual coding and speech acoustic features, it is difficult for the model to learn the joint distribution between the two from limited data.

% neglecting the critical need for natural word-level speech generation. This gap severely limits real-time communication between hearing-impaired and normal-hearing individuals, particularly in educational and social contexts. While lip-reading speech synthesis methods like LipVoicer \cite{yemini2023lipvoicer} leverage lip motions alone, they fail to model the complementary hand coding in CS.
 % Thus, the primary motivation of this study is \textbf{to undertake the first attempt at directly decoding Chinese CS videos into word-level speech}, as illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:overview} (b).




