Recent advances in multimodal generation have demonstrated that integrating semantic reasoning capabilities from vision-language models (VLMs) can significantly enhance complex generation tasks, such as text-guided image synthesis with interleaved control \cite{mi2025think,chen2025multimodal}. Inspired by this paradigm, we believe that the fine-grained visual understanding inherent in CSR can serve as a semantic compensator for CSV2S generation. As illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:motivation}(b), our framework introduces a shared visual processor that bridges CSR and CSV2S tasks. This processor operates as a two-way translator: during CSR, it extracts linguistic semantics from spatiotemporal lip-hand patterns; for CSV2S, it maps these semantics to guide speech synthesis while preserving cuer identity. The key innovation lies in explicitly modeling the \textbf{semantic compensation flow}, where CSR-derived phoneme-level constraints reduce potential ambiguities in word-level speech generation, overcoming the challenges of fine-grained semantic correlation modeling.

