% The core insight of our study is that \textbf{the fine-grained visual semantic analysis provided by the CS Recognition (CSR) can effectively compensate for the V2S generation model’s insufficient understanding of CS semantics.} As illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:motivation}, a shared visual processor acts as a bridge, enabling CSR to assist V2S generation in modeling fine-grained semantic correlations between CS visual representation and speech, thereby enhancing the overall performance of V2S generation.


To address the above challenges, building upon the semantic compensation paradigm, in this work, we propose \textbf{UniCUE}, the first unified framework that bridges CSR and CSV2S tasks through three specific components:
\textbf{Firstly},
Unlike prior CSR methods \cite{liu2024computation,liu2023cross} that process lip and hand modalities independently, relying solely on raw video embeddings, UniCUE is equipped with a pose-aware visual processor that simultaneously processes both modalities.
This allows UniCUE to generate a mixed visual representation by combining video embeddings and pose embeddings.
 This mixed setup not only achieves fine-grained spatiotemporal modeling for hand-preceding phenomenon but also enhances robustness to cuer-specific CS expression.
\textbf{Secondly}, to enhance the semantic alignment between visual cues and spoken content, we introduce a semantic alignment pool. 
This mechanism aligns the video latent space and the pose latent space with linguistic semantics via contrastive learning, enabling the model to capture complex semantic correlations and improve the accuracy of the generated speech.
\textbf{Thirdly}, 
 we incorporate the visual processor from CSR into the diffusion-based CSV2S model, using an VisioPhonetic Adapter (VPA) to translate fine-grained representation into diffusion-compatible codes. Based on the identity correlation between speech and face portrait \cite{rong2024seeing}, this design enables our diffusion decoder to not only effectively integrate the semantic information conveyed by the visual cues but also capture the unique characteristics of the cuer identity in the visual frames, thereby enhancing accurate cuer-specific speech generation.

To evaluate UniCUE on hearing-impaired individuals, we collect CS data from 8 hearing-impaired cuers. Additionally, we extend the Mandarin Chinese CS dataset \cite{lei2024bridge} by incorporating data from 8 hearing-impaired and 6 normal-hearing cuers (individuals who perform CS), resulting in a total of 11,282 CS videos. The results on this dataset demonstrate that our method not only achieves accurate linguistic understanding but also preserves the cuer's characteristics.


The main contributions of this work can be summarized as:
\begin{itemize}
    \item We propose the first CSV2S generation framework by constructing a new unified multimodal system that integrating CSR capabilities to enhance CSV2S.
    \item We propose a pose-aware visual processor and a semantic alignment pool to enhance fine-grained, semantically aligned visual representations, and introduce an VPA module to convert fine-grained semantic information into understandable coding for the speech synthesis model.
    % \item We extend a larger Mandarin Chinese CS dataset comprising 11,282 high-resolution videos from 8 hearing-impaired and 6 normal-hearing cuers.
    \item We establish a new Mandarin Chinese CS dataset comprising 8 hearing-impaired and 6 normal-hearing cuers. Experimental results demonstrate that our UniCUE framework outperforms the single CSV2S model in terms of speech consistency, accuracy, and time efficiency.
\end{itemize}

