\section{Related Work}

\subsection{Video-to-Speech Generation}
V2S involves generating natural-sounding speech synchronized with a silent video of a person talking. A key challenge in this area is performing complex visual-speech modeling under the limited data. To address this challenge, Uni-Dubbing \cite{lei2024uni} incorporates modality alignment during the pre-training phase on multimodal datasets and then fine-tunes using both multimodal and single-modality audio data. Similarly, Kefalas \textit{et al.} \cite{kefalas2024large} leverage abundant audio-only datasets to pre-train the speech encoder and decoder, subsequently fine-tuning with paired speech-video data.
Instead of relying on external data, some works \cite{kim2023lip,yemini2023lipvoicer,gupta2024visatronic} have found that incorporating text transcripts plays a significant role in enhancing V2S generation. For example, Kim \textit{et al.} \cite{kim2023lip} propose guiding the model using multimodal supervision, \textit{i.e.,} text and speech, to complement insufficient word representations via multi-task learning. 
%LipVoicer \cite{yemini2023lipvoicer} employs a pre-trained lip-reading network to predict the spoken text and then guides speech generation through a classifier-guidance mechanism. %Meanwhile, Visatronic \cite{gupta2024visatronic} embeds vision, text, and speech directly into a common subspace of a transformer model and uses an autoregressive loss to learn a generative model of discretized mel-spectrograms conditioned on cuer videos and transcripts of their speech.
In contrast to directly using text transcripts, we innovatively utilize the shared semantic correlations between visual-text and visual-audio in CSR and CSV2S. 

\subsection{Cued Speech Recognition}
CS is an augmented lip-reading system enhanced by hand coding, offering significant support to the hearing-impaired community. While CSR task focuses on converting CS videos into linguistic texts, leveraging both lips and hands as distinct modalities that provide complementary information \cite{liu2019pilot,papadimitriou2021fully}. 
Existing CSR methods often process lip and hand features independently, followed by feature fusion for content recognition \cite{liu2020re,liu2024computation,liu2023cross,zhang2023cuing}.  Due to the asynchronous nature of lip and hand movements, achieving effective fusion remains a challenging problem. Liu \textit{et al.} \cite{liu2020re} introduced a re-synchronization procedure to align hand features with lip features, addressing the temporal mismatch. While a transformer-based cross-modal mutual learning framework has been developed to facilitate multimodal interaction \cite{liu2023cross,liu2024computation}. Zhang \textit{et al.} \cite{zhang2023cuing} further explored privacy risks in the training process by applying federated learning. 
In contrast, our approach takes whole video frames as input that models lip and hand features simultaneously, avoiding the complexities of feature fusion. Moreover, we propose a pose-aware visual processor to enhance feature representation between the two modalities, thereby improving recognition accuracy.


\subsection{Unified Understanding and Generation}
Recent efforts in unifying understanding and generation tasks can be broadly categorized into two paradigms. The first paradigm integrates visual-language understanding with external generative models (\textit{e.g.,} diffusion models) to achieve multimodal generation \cite{wu2023next,dong2023dreamllm,jin2023unified,li2024mini,sun2024generative,ge2024seed,ge2024divot}. For instance, works like \cite{jin2023unified,li2024mini} leverage large language models (LLMs) for semantic comprehension while relying on diffusion models \cite{rombach2022high,podell2023sdxl} for high-quality image synthesis. 
The second paradigm focuses on optimizing a LLMs-based foundation models through next-token prediction objectives for both visual understanding and generation \cite{yu2023scaling,sun2023emu,zhou2024transfusion,wu2024vila,fang2024puma,wu2024janus,chen2025janus}. For instance, Transfusion \cite{zhou2024transfusion} unifies the processes of image understanding and image generation within a transformer. This approach not only inherits the strong comprehension ability of language models but also preserves the detailed information of images, achieving better controllable generation results in text-to-image.
Despite their success, these frameworks predominantly address visual-text scenarios, leveraging understanding for speech generation is unexplored. 
In contrast to prior works, we present the first unified framework that bridges visual understanding and speech generation.

