 \subsection{UniCUE}
 \label{subsec:unified}
 To achieve accurate CSV2S generation, the proposed framework need simultaneously resolve two critical challenges: (1) \textbf{semantic understanding} of the semantic correlations between visual cues and speech content, and (2) \textbf{speech synthesis} that preserves cuer-specific characteristics and temporal alignment. 
Inspired by the auxiliary benefits of unified understanding and generation for multimodal controllable image synthesis \cite{wu2023next,dong2023dreamllm}, we design a unified 
architecture that integrates CSR and CSV2S, enabling CSV2S with understanding capability improvement through shared visual feature representations.
As illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:framework}, the framework operates via two pathways.


\textbf{CSR: Fine-grained Visual Cues Understanding.} 
 Given a CS video \( I_v \) and its corresponding pose \( I_p \) (extracted via OpenPose \cite{8765346}), a pose-aware visual processor extracts the embedding of the visual mixture \( Z_{mv} \). The visual embedding \( Z_{mv} \) is first transcribed into linguistic text via a transformer-based text decoder $D_T$ to model a fine-grained spatiotemporal visual representation space. The process can be denoted as: $ T_p = D_T(Z_{mv})$, where $T_p$ is the predicted text tokens.
% \begin{equation}
%     T_p = D_T(Z_{mv}),
% \end{equation}
% where $T_p$ is the predicted text tokens.
The learning objective of the CSR path is to predict text tokens \( T_{p} \) as closely as possible to the ground truth text tokens \( T_{g} \):%\vspace{-5pt}
\begin{equation}
      \mathcal{L}_{R} = - \sum_{i=1}^{N} T_p^i \log(T_g^i),
      \label{eq:CSR}
\end{equation}
where \( T_p^i \) and \( T_g^i \) are the \( i \)-th token of the predicted tokens and ground truth, respectively, and \( N \) is the total number of tokens.
By transcribing cross-cuer visual cues into linguistic text, CSR path establishes phoneme-level semantic grounding for the visual embedding $Z_{mv}$, which equips $Z_{mv}$ with cuer-adaptive, fine-grained semantic understanding capabilities. 

  % enabling it to enhance the sequential CSV2S to encode correlations between visual cues and speech. 

\textbf{CSV2S: Cuer-specific Speech Synthesis.}
Based on the correlation between face portrait and speech \cite{rong2024seeing}, a latent diffusion model (LDM) \cite{rombach2022high} is trained to synthesize cuer-specific speech conditioned on the learned visual embedding \( Z_{mv} \).
Specifically, a pretrained VAE encoder compresses ground-truth mel-spectrograms into latent codes \( Z_s \), which are then corrupted into \( Z_s^t \) by sequentially injecting Gaussian noise \( \epsilon \) over \( t \) time steps: 
% $Z_s^t := \alpha_t * Z_s + (1 - \alpha_t) * \epsilon$, where $\alpha_t$ represents the noise level at the $t$ time step.
\begin{equation}
    Z_s^t := \alpha_t * Z_s + (1 - \alpha_t) * \epsilon,
\end{equation}
where $\alpha_t$ represents the noise level at the $t$ time step.
The noised \( Z_s^t \) is then input into the LDM \cite{rombach2022high} to learn the distribution of latent speech codes under the visual condition \( Z_{mv} \). The objective of the CSV2S generation path can be formulated as:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{G} := \mathbb{E}_{Z_s^t \, , \, Z_{mv} \, , \, \epsilon \, , \, t} \left[ \left\Vert \epsilon - \mathcal{M}(Z_s^t, Z_{mv}, t) \right\Vert^2_2 \right],
\label{eq:V2S}
\end{equation}
where \( \mathcal{M} \) represents the LDM.
By training LDM, visual information is interacted with latent speech codes, enabling the generated speech to accurately match the dynamic changes in the visual content while preserving the naturalness of personalized speech.


\textbf{UniCUE: Unified Understanding and Generation.}
The CSR path learns fine-grained visual embeddings \( Z_{mv} \) by performing linguistic text recognition, which are then used as conditional inputs for the CSV2S path, enabling CSV2S to leverage the fine-grained visual understanding for accurate speech generation. This shared mechanism in our unified framework not only reduces information loss but also avoids error propagation that might arise from intermediate text conversions. Therefore, CSV2S can capture cue-specific acoustic features while maintaining linguistic accuracy and temporal consistency, generating cuer-specific speech outputs.

% Through the shared visual conditioning \( Z_{mv} \),  CSV2S path is able to capture cue-specific acoustic features while maintaining linguistic accuracy and temporal consistency, making full use of visual information to generate speech that aligns with the target audio.

% The LDM leverages the shared visual condition \( Z_{mv} \)
%   to capture cuer-specific acoustic characteristics while maintaining linguistic accuracy and temporal consistency.




 \subsection{Pose-aware Visual Processor}
\label{subsec:visual processor}
Considering the spatiotemporal correlation between hand coding, lip reading, and their semantic content,
both CSV2S and CSR require precise localization of lip and hand keypoints.
This necessity motivates the visual encoder to capture fine-grained pose features. To address this, we develop a pose-aware visual processor to construct mixed visual representations by synergistically integrating pose and video embeddings, as shown in Figure \ref{fig:framework}.

Specifically, given the video input \( I_v \) and pose input \( I_p \), a tokenizer first splits them into patch tokens.
Let the ViT-based image encoder be denoted as \( E_I \). We extract the video features \( Z_v \) and pose features \( Z_p \) from the given video tokens \( T_v \) and pose tokens \( T_p \), respectively:
\begin{equation}
    Z_v = E_I(T_v), \quad Z_p = E_I(T_p), 
\end{equation}
where \( Z_v \in \mathbb{R}^{L \times D} \) and \( Z_p \in \mathbb{R}^{L \times D} \). Here, \( L \) represents the number of visual patch tokens and \( D \) denotes the dimension of visual embedding.
The video features \( Z_v \) and pose features \( Z_p \) are then concatenated along channel dimension, apply a trainable projector layer to form the mixed visual embedding \( Z_{mv} \):
\begin{equation}
    Z_{mv} = \text{MLP}(\text{ConCat}(Z_v, Z_p)),
\end{equation}
where \( \text{MLP} \) refers to the projection layer.
The CSR path supervises the training of the pose-aware visual processor, guiding it to adapt to cuer-specific CS expressions and learn fine-grained semantic representations.

 % we design a semantic alignment pool to individual alignment of video and pose features with text embeddings to capture complementary cues.

 \subsection{Semantic Alignment Pool }
 \label{subsec:alignment}
 To further enhance fine-grained semantic consistency between visual representation and linguistic content, we propose a semantic alignment mechanism that synchronizes visual, pose, and textual modalities through contrastive learning. Specifically, a ViT-based text encoder  
first projects tokens of ground-truth transcripts \( T_g \)
into embeddings \( Z_t \), sharing the same latent space with visual \( Z_v \) and pose \( Z_p \) features extracted by the pose-aware processor. 
We formulate the alignment objective as a multi-modal embedding alignment Loss:
%\vspace{-3pt}
\begin{equation}
        \mathcal{L}_{v \leftrightarrow t} = 1 - \text{Cos}(Z_v, Z_t), \quad
      \mathcal{L}_{p \leftrightarrow t} = 1 - \text{Cos}(Z_p, Z_t) , 
\end{equation}
%\vspace{-3pt}
where \(\text{Cos($\cdot$,$\cdot$)}\) denotes the cosine similarity. The total semantic alignment loss is calculated via:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L_S} = \mathcal{L}_{v \leftrightarrow t}+\mathcal{L}_{p\leftrightarrow t}.
    \label{eq:CL}
\end{equation}
By individually aligning video and pose features with text embeddings, the model can effectively integrate complementary cues, enhancing its ability to understand visual cues for speech generation.


