\section{Experiment}
\subsection{Dataset}
To further enhance the model's generalization ability and validate its practical effectiveness in facilitating communication for hearing-impaired individuals, we establish a new Chinese CS dataset by incorporating CS data from 2 normal-hearing and 8 hearing-impaired cuers with 4 normal-hearing cuers data in \cite{lei2024bridge}, increasing the total number of sentences in the dataset to 11,282.
A comparison between our dataset and existing CS datasets is presented in Table \ref{tab:dataset}. Specifically, we invited 2 normal-hearing cuers and 8 hearing-impaired cuers to simultaneously utter and encode a set of text sentences provided by \cite{lei2024bridge}. The 10 cuers underwent systematic training to ensure they could perform Mandarin Chinese CS fluently and accurately. 
It is important to note that our dataset was collected with the explicit consent of all cuers and is available for open-source use.
We use CS data from 6 normal-hearing cuers to train the model, splitting this data into training and test sets in a $0.95:0.05$ ratio to ensure effective model training and preliminary validation. To more comprehensively evaluate the practical effectiveness in facilitating communication for hearing-impaired individuals, we included \textbf{all the CS data from 8 hearing-impaired cuers in the test set}.

\subsection{Implementation Details}
\textbf{Architecture Details.}
To construct the CSV2S path, we employ a speech generation pipeline consisting of a VAE encoder and decoder, an LDM, and a Vocoder from AudioLDM \cite{liu2023audioldm}. We initialize the pipeline by loading the pre-trained weights of the VAE and LDM.
For the CSR path, we utilize a conditional translation LLM, MBart \cite{liu2020multilingual}, which comprises a tokenizer, an image encoder, a text encoder, and a text decoder. The pipeline is initialized by loading the pre-trained weights with multi-language corpus\footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/facebook/mbart-large-50}}.

\noindent\textbf{Training Details.}
We extract CS pose information—including hands, face, and body—from videos using OpenPose\footnote{\url{https://github.com/CMU-Perceptual-Computing-Lab/openpose}}.  
Raw video frames and pose images are resized to \(256 \times 256\), while the audio is resampled to 16 kHz and converted into mel-spectrograms. The entire learning framework is implemented in PyTorch, and all experiments are conducted on an NVIDIA RTX A6000. We use the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of \(5 \times 10^{-4}\) for end-to-end training, setting the mini-batch size to 2.

\noindent\textbf{Comparison Methods.}
To validate the effectiveness of our framework,
we conduct extensive comparisons between our method \textbf{UniCUE} (CSV2S with CSR-transferred information) and the following methods:
\textbf{1) our single CSV2S:} an end-to-end trained CSV2S without the shared CSR information;
 \textbf{2) our CSR:} comprising of the pose-aware visual processor and text decoder to output CS linguistic text (shown in Figure 3);
\textbf{3) SOTA CSR methods:} including CMML \cite{liu2023cross}, EcoCued \cite{liu2024computation}, and various combinations of CNNs and transformers, such as FLASH \cite{hua2022transformer}, Linformer \cite{wang2020linformer}, Performer \cite{choromanski2020rethinking}, and Cosformer \cite{zhen2022cosformer}.
For fairness, we reproduce these CSR methods on our dataset using the same experimental setup described in their respective papers. 
Since CSR methods only output phoneme-level transcriptions, we propose a \textbf{two-stage conversion pipeline to synthesis speech}, as illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:infer} 
 (a). The process consists of: \textbf{1)} converting CSR outputs into Chinese pinyin sequences using rule-based CS patterns, followed by translating the pinyin into Chinese characters with \textit{QWen} toolkit\footnote{\href{https://github.com/QwenLM/Qwen}{https://github.com/QwenLM/Qwen}};\textbf{2)} generating the final waveforms using SOTA TTS model F5-TTS \cite{chen2024f5} with reference speech.


