\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
To evaluate the performance of the synthesized speech, we employ three categories of metrics focusing on linguistic accuracy, audio-visual synchronization, and speech quality.
For \textbf{linguistic accuracy}, we measure the \textit{Word Error Rate} (WER) between the recognized text of generated speech and ground truth transcripts. 
The transcripts are normalized by removing punctuation and converting numbers to words prior to alignment.  
To measure the \textbf{audio-visual synchronization} between the generated speech and the corresponding video, we utilize SyncNet~\cite{Chung16a} and report two key metrics: \textit{LSE-D} (the temporal distance between audio and video) and \textit{LSE-C} (the confidence score). These values are computed using the pre-trained SyncNet model\footnote{\url{https://github.com/joonson/syncnet-python}}.  
For \textbf{speech quality}, \textit{Fréchet Audio Distance} (FAD) \cite{kilgour2018fr} is employed to assess the naturalness of generated speech by measuring the distribution divergence between real and synthesized audio in a pretrained VGGish embedding space~\cite{Hershey2017CNN}.

