\subsection{User Study}

To holistically evaluate the perceptual quality of synthesized speech, we conduct a user study. In this experiment, test samples were randomly selected from the outputs of each cuer, and 10 volunteers were invited to rate the generated speech in three perceptual dimensions on 5-point Likert scales: \textbf{Accuracy} (1: Unintelligible, 5: Perfect), \textbf{Quality} (1: Artificial, 5: Human-like), and \textbf{Synchronization} (1: Desynchronized, 5: Perfectly aligned). Four systems were compared: the SOTA CSR baseline (CMML \cite{liu2023cross}), our CSR, CSV2S, and the proposed UniCUE. 
Based on the mean and variance values presented in Figure \ref{fig:user_study}, UniCUE demonstrates statistically significant superiority across all metrics. These results confirm that the unified framework successfully bridges the gap between visual understanding and speech generation, outperforming both combined pipelines and task-specific baselines in human perception.

