\textbf{Computational Efficiency.}
To quantify the efficiency gains of our unified framework, we conduct time cost evaluations across both training and inference stage.
\noindent\textbf{(a) Training Efficiency.}
We compared the training performance of CSV2S and UniCUE to evaluate the training efficiency of our proposed unified framework. As shown in the Figure \ref{fig:train_cost}, the training loss of UniCUE decreases rapidly to a stable value in fewer epochs, indicating that the introduction of fine-grained understanding ability accelerates the learning process of the speech generation model. Moreover, the validation loss also decreases concurrently with less fluctuation, demonstrating improved model robustness.
\noindent\textbf{(b) Real-time Inference. }
To validate the real-time performance of the model, this study further compares the inference efficiency of combined(our CSR and TTS) and unified CSV2S. As an end-to-end CSV2S generation framework, our UniCUE significantly reduces the inference time (4.15s), achieving a 40\% reduction compared to the traditional CSR + TTS serial approach (6.93s), thereby confirming the advantages of the unified architecture in efficiency optimization.








\section{Conclusion}
This work introduces UniCUE, the first unified framework that integrates Cued Speech recognition (CSR) and video-to-speech (CSV2S) generation for Chinese Mandarin CS, addressing the critical challenges of bridging communication barriers between the hearing-impaired and normal-hearing communities.
By integrating CS recognition with diffusion-based speech generation, our framework achieves fine-grained visual-semantic alignment and precise temporal synchronization, even with limited CS training data. Key innovations, such as the pose-aware visual processor for detailed visual embedding, the semantic alignment pool for robust visual-text mapping, and the VPA for injecting CSR knowledge into the diffusion decoder, collectively enable speech synthesis that preserves both linguistic accuracy and temporal consistency.
Experimental results demonstrate that our unified approach not only outperforms SOTA CSR methods in semantic consistency but also achieves strong performance in temporal alignment. Furthermore, the expanded Chinese CS dataset establishes a solid foundation for future research.

