\section{Conclusion}
This study explores the integration of AI-driven behavioral generative agents into practical energy operations. By embedding LLM-based agents within dynamic operational simulations, we demonstrate the potential of AI agents to credibly replicate realistic human decision-making in complex engineering contexts.

Our approach presents three notable advantages. First, it provides \textit{efficiency and flexibility}. Compared to traditional demand-side studies involving human participants \citep{AUS14data, Ram24DERA, Xie17ECoupon}, our method rapidly generates comprehensive data capturing both the operational decisions and their underlying reasoning through detailed textual outputs. This allows for efficient iterative experiments where agents can be re-prompted, facilitating deeper insights into evolving consumer preferences and behaviors. 
Second, our framework effectively balances the \textit{complexity-computation trade-off}, distilling intricate real-world energy management scenarios \citep{AWS24LLMPowerSystem} into controlled yet realistic experiments that remain computationally feasible. Third, our method enables \textit{rich behavioral analysis with limited historical data}, circumventing the need for extensive fine-tuning of LLMs on pre-existing datasets \citep{hewitt24socialsciencellm}. Instead, we utilize carefully crafted prompts to simulate diverse consumer personas and capture adaptive responses to rare, high-impact events like blackouts.

The practical implications for energy planners and policymakers are significant. Behavioral generative agents serve as valuable decision-support tools, enabling stakeholders to simulate how different consumer archetypes might react to new energy policies, such as dynamic pricing schemes or emergency demand-response initiatives. These virtual testbeds offer opportunities to proactively identify barriers to consumer adoption, mitigate equity concerns, and anticipate unintended behavioral consequences, thereby refining strategies before costly real-world implementations.

In conclusion, our study highlights a promising and practical approach to leveraging behavioral generative agents within dynamic energy systems. By demonstrating a method that is both experimentally rigorous and computationally manageable, we emphasize the potential of \textit{Homo Silicus} as a complementary tool for advancing our understanding of consumer behavior in complex socio-technical environments, thereby facilitating more effective and inclusive transitions toward sustainable energy futures



