\begin{abstract}
This paper proposes an upper-limb exoskeleton teleoperation system based on magnetorheological (MR) clutches, aiming to improve operational accuracy and enhance the immersive experience during lunar sampling tasks. Conventional exoskeleton teleoperation systems commonly employ active force feedback solutions, such as servo motors, which typically suffer from high system complexity and increased energy consumption. Furthermore, force feedback devices utilizing motors and gear reducers generally compromise backdrivability and pose safety risks to operators due to active force output.
To address these limitations, we propose a semi-active force feedback strategy based on MR clutches. Dynamic magnetic field control enables precise adjustment of joint stiffness and damping, thereby providing smooth and high-resolution force feedback.
The designed MR clutch exhibits outstanding performance across key metrics, achieving a torque-to-mass ratio (TMR) of 93.6\,N$\cdot$m/kg, a torque-to-volume ratio (TVR) of 4.05$\times$10$^{5}$\,N$\cdot$m/m$^{3}$, and a torque-to-power ratio (TPR) of 4.15\,N$\cdot$m/W. Notably, the TMR represents an improvement of approximately 246\% over a representative design in prior work\cite{Pisetskiy2021}. Experimental results validate the system's capability to deliver high-fidelity force feedback. Overall, the proposed system presents a promising solution for deep-space teleoperation with strong potential for real-world deployment in future missions.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Telerobotics and Teleoperation, Human-Robot Collaboration, Exoskeleton, Force Feedback, MR Clutch
\end{IEEEkeywords}


\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{t}{eleoperated} robotic technology has advanced rapidly in response to the growing demand for remote operations in space exploration in recent years\cite{Guo2024}. Feedback from the robot to the human operator is essential for safe and precise interaction with the environment\cite{Huang2025,Feizi2023,MohandOusaid2020,Cabibihan2021}. For example, during lunar surface sampling, a reliable force feedback mechanism allows the operator to perceive force conditions in a low-gravity environment, minimizing sample damage and enabling fine adjustments. To meet these requirements, wearable exoskeleton-based teleoperation has emerged as a promising solution to achieve intuitive control and effective force feedback\cite{Ma2015,Buongiorno2018,Varghese2020,Ben2025}.

Current force feedback mechanisms are primarily based on active drives, such as cable-driven, pneumatic, or motor-driven systems. 
Moein Forouhar et al. developed a lightweight exoskeleton using Bowden cables\cite{Forouhar2024} and Rong Su et al. proposed a 7-degree-of-freedom (DOF) wearable exoskeleton\cite{Su2021}. However, both designs lack joint-level force feedback at the exoskeleton. 
Cosima du Pasquier et al. introduced Haptiknit, a flexible exoskeleton with pneumatic actuators\cite{duPasquier2024} , John Nassour et al. developed a soft pneumatic elbow exoskeleton\cite{Nassour2021} and Li et al. propose a teleoperation system using soft robotic principles\cite{Li2019}. The use of pneumatic actuators to provide force feedback can enhance wearablity.
Nevertheless, the inherent response delay of pneumatic systems poses a significant limitation. Francesco Porcini et al. designed a motor-based remote control architecture for exoskeletons\cite{Porcini2020}, but the operator was constrained to a fixed platform, resulting in limited mobility. Moreover, motor-reducer-based force feedback devices often suffer from a loss of backdrivability, which not only reduces the system's transparency but also adds extra weight and power consumption to the exoskeleton.    
For instance, Adam Zoss et al. implemented electric motor actuation in the lower extremity exoskeleton BLEEX for power assistance; however, the system exhibited a high average power consumption of 510 W, and the weight of the electric-driven joints amounted to 4.1 kg\cite{Zoss2006}.
In response to these challenges, semi-active drive methods has emerged as a promising alternative, offering improved safety, mobility, and energy efficiency.

MRG (Magnetorheological grease) is an smart material characterized by its ability to dynamically adjust the viscosity and stiffness in response to externally applied magnetic fields, enabling precise and adaptive control of the output damping force\cite{Raj2021,Park2010}. MR clutches based on MRG exhibit several distinct advantages. Firstly, they offer an exceptionally high torque density. The prototype developed in this study, with a mass of 450 g, achieves a peak locking torque of 42\,N$\cdot$m, allowing MR clutches to generate high torque output while ensuring excellent wearability. Secondly, a force feedback gain strategy is proposed to enhance haptic sensitivity during teleoperation. By dynamically scaling the output torque of the MR clutches in proportion to the interaction force, the system enables a more intuitive perception of remote contact events. This strategy is implemented in a semi-active single-arm exoskeleton and validated through teleoperation tasks, demonstrating its effectiveness in improving contact discrimination and force awareness. Furthermore, the MR clutch consumes only 10W of power at peak locking torque output, and the MR materials have the ability to respond dynamically in milliseconds, these advantages make MR clutches particularly suitable for practical engineering applications\cite{DeVicente2011,Carlson2000}.

In conclusion, a semi-active single-arm exoskeleton based on MR clutches is proposed in this study to enable high-precision force feedback during the teleoperation of robotic arms. Beyond achieving effective master-slave tracking control, the system dynamically adjusts the damping level of the MR clutch in real time, enabling high-fidelity reproduction of external forces and collisions encountered by the robotic arm. This force-aware capability significantly enhances the teleoperation experience, especially in complex and unstructured environments. The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section II presents the overall system design and methodology, including the hardware and software architecture for teleoperation and force feedback, the structural design and performance evaluation of the MR clutch, and the proposed control strategy. Section III details the experimental setup and results, covering teleoperation tasks as well as force feedback validation through obstacle avoidance and stiffness recognition experiments. Finally, Section IV concludes the paper and outlines future research directions.

\section{System Design and Methodology}

\subsection{Teleoperation and Force Feedback Architecture}
The system framework comprises three primary components: the master end, the slave end, and the control end. Fig. 1 illustrates the design of teleoperation and force feedback workflow.

The master end is an operator wearing a single-arm upper-limb exoskeleton based on MR clutches. The exoskeleton incorporates four joints corresponding to the three DOFs of the shoulder—abduction/adduction, flexion/extension, and internal/external rotation—and one DOF of the elbow (flexion/extension)\cite{Yan2014}. Each joint integrates an MR clutch and an angular encoder. The MR clutches provide damping force for force feedback, while the angular encoders capture joint angle data. An inertial measurement unit (IMU) is mounted on the dorsum of the operator's hand to acquire hand orientation data. The angular encoder and IMU data are transmitted to the control end via a microcontroller unit (MCU). The MR clutch driving module is mounted on the operator's back to improve wearability and motion flexibility.
\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/F1.pdf}    
    \caption{Design of teleoperation and force feedback workflow.}
\end{figure}

The slave end employs a 7-DOF robotic arm (Model K1, Unitree Robotics). A force sensor is mounted at the end-effector of the K1 arm to measure interaction forces. The original gripper of the K1 has been replaced by a dexterous hand (uHand, Hiwonder), enabling a wider range of manipulation tasks. The uHand is teleoperated via its companion motion-sensing glove to perform grasping operations. Further details of the dexterous hand and motion-sensing glove are provided in Supplementary Figure S1.

At the control end, forward kinematics is applied to process the joint angle and IMU data into the position and orientation of the master hand's end-effector relative to the shoulder joint. This information is then mapped to the position and orientation of the K1's end-effector relative to its base. Inverse kinematics is subsequently used to drive the servos of the K1, enabling it to follow the motion of the exoskeleton. Simultaneously, force data from the K1's end-effector is processed and converted into an excitation current signal applied to the MR clutch coils. This current modulates the magnetic field within the MR clutches, thereby adjusting the damping force accordingly. When the force data is relatively small, the MR clutches exert a lower level of damping force, allowing the operator to move the exoskeleton with low resistance. However, as the force increases (e.g., when the K1 arm collides with a rigid object), the MR clutches generate greater damping force to restrict joint rotation, thereby enabling the operator to perceive a corresponding feedback force.

\subsection{Design and Evaluation of MR Clutch}

\subsubsection{Structural Design of MR Clutch}

\begin{figure*}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/F2.pdf}
    \caption{Structure, working principle, and magnetic field analysis of the MR clutch. (a) Cutaway view of MR clutch structure. (b) Principle of MR bearings. (c) Magnetic flux density simulation with 1.3 A current input. The illustration displays the symmetric magnetic circuit formed in the construction of the MR clutch and the sites of the three probes. (d) Magnetic flux density of the three probes under various currents. (e) MR clutch testing apparatus and platform. (f) Maximum locking torque of the MR clutch under different currents.}
\end{figure*}

The internal structure of the designed MR clutch is shown in Fig. 2(a). The stator part consists of a sealed cup, stator ring, and aluminum alloy base 1, which are fixed to the parent link of the joint. The rotor part comprises the rotor ring and aluminum base 2, which are fixed to the child link of the joint. MR bearings and coils are arranged between the rotor and stator, a dual-bearing structure in the form of “MR bearing - coil - MR bearing” is adopted, which maximizes the utilization of the magnetic field generated by the coil and significantly enhances the locking torque of the MR clutch.

The key component of the MR clutch is the MR bearing. The MR bearing consists of a deep groove ball bearing and MRG distributed in the gap spaces inside the bearing. MRG is made by mixing high-viscosity lubricating grease with iron powder, approximately 0.5\,$\mu$m in particle size, and stirring them together. First, the beaker containing the high-viscosity lubricating grease is placed in a water bath and heated to 80°C. Then, a stirrer is used to mix the grease at 500 rpm for ten minutes. After that, iron powder is weighed and added to the stirred high-viscosity grease, and the mixture is stirred at 800 rpm for 60 minutes at 80°C until the iron powder is fully dispersed in the grease. To manufacture the MR bearing, the MRG is injected into the deep groove ball bearing when the bearing cover is open. Afterward, the bearing is placed in a vacuum oven for 5 minutes to remove air bubbles and saturate the bearing space with MRG. The bearing cover is sealed after multiple repeated operations.

When exposed to an external magnetic field, the MRG transitions rapidly from a free-flowing liquid to a semi-solid or solid state within milliseconds, demonstrating significant rheological control. As shown in Fig. 2(b), in the absence of a magnetic field, the magnetic particles are randomly distributed, resulting in minimal damping. This allows the MR bearings to rotate freely, and the exoskeleton wearer's joint movement is not constrained by locking torque, leading to no significant perception of force feedback.
Upon application of a magnetic field, the magnetic particles align into a chain-like structure along the magnetic field direction.  The MR bearing experiences a controllable locking torque that is positively correlated with the magnetic field density.  By adjusting the magnetic field density, the feedback force can be linearly regulated within a certain range. When the magnetic flux density reaches saturation, the locking torque of the MR bearing is limited by the hysteresis characteristics of the material. At this point, due to the rigid locking of the chain-like structure within the narrow gap, the MR bearing essentially loses its rotational degrees of freedom. This condition simulates a scenario where the robotic arm's end-effector is subjected to abnormal impact loads, such as a transient overload caused by operator error.  In such cases, the high locking torque can effectively suppress the joint movement of the exoskeleton wearer, preventing structural damage to the follower robotic arm caused by impact loads and significantly enhancing the system's safety.

\subsubsection{Magnetic Simulation and Performance Analysis}

The magnetic field simulation and analysis were carried out to visualize the magnetic field distribution and to verify the feasibility of the designed MR clutch. As defined in the software COMSOL Multiphysics, material properties were assigned accordingly for all components. The current within the coil ranges from 0 to 2.0 A, yielding magnetic field distribution and trends. As shown in Fig. 2(c), three probes are set in the MR clutch to measure the magnetic field density. Probes 1 and 2 were placed in the stator ring and rotor ring. Probe 3 was set to measure the magnetic field density of the MRG in the bearing gaps. As shown in Fig. 2(d), the magnetic field density of the three probes increased with the current, and the magnetic field was close to saturation with an input current of 1.3 A. As a result, the magnetic field distributions and corresponding value changes show that the magnetic field to which MR bearings are subjected can be controlled effectively.

To further investigate the relationship between excitation current and the locking torque of the MR clutch, an experimental setup was established, as illustrated in Fig. 2(e). The system consists of three main components. On the right is a servo motor with a rated torque of 100\,N$\cdot$m. At the center is a torque sensor with a measurement range of 100\,N$\cdot$m and an accuracy of 0.1\%, which outputs analog voltage signals for data acquisition and analysis. The MR clutch is positioned on the left. These components are mounted on an optical platform using aluminum alloy brackets. They are connected via couplings to ensure alignment and mechanical compliance. The torque data acquisition and motor control signals are handled by a controller, while the excitation current for the MR clutch is supplied by a DC power source. During the experiment, the controller regulates the motor speed, which drives the coupling connected to the MR clutch. The torque sensor measures the rotational torque transmitted through the coupling. The damping force is adjusted by the current in the coil, which is controlled by a DC power supply.


The relationship between the excitation current and the locking torque is shown in Fig. 2(f). The original data represents the average of numerous experiments. When the excitation current is 0 A, the MR clutch exhibits an idle torque of 0.2\,N$\cdot$m. The locking torque of the MR clutch increases with the excitation current and reaches a maximum value of 42.12\,N$\cdot$m at 1.3 A due to magnetic saturation. Beyond this point, further increases in excitation current produce negligible changes in the locking torque. Therefore, this study focuses on the variation of excitation current within the range of 0-1.3 A. The experimental data are fitted using the Hill function.The data in Fig. 2(f) are fitted using the Hill function. The basic form of the Hill function is as follows:
\begin{equation}
    \label{equation_1}
    f(x)=\frac{V_{\max } \cdot x^{n}}{K^{n}+x^{n}}   
\end{equation}

Here, $V_{\max }$=54.28, $K$=0.66, and $n$=1.96. $x$ represents the magnitude of the excitation current, and $f(x)$ represents the locking torque of MR clutch. Repetition testing was performed to conduct an error analysis on the fitted curve. The error band in Fig. 2(f) represents the maximum deviation observed in the repeated experiments, providing a conservative estimate of the potential error range. To quantitatively assess the performance, several error metrics were calculated based on the maximum observed deviation. The Mean Absolute Error (MAE) is 0.94, and the Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) is 1.05. Considering the entire range of values, the Normalized Root Mean Square Error (nRMSE) is as low as 2.46\%, indicating that the fitted curve effectively describes the output characteristics of the MR clutch. The analysis of the fitted curve helps us to precisely control the output torque of the MR clutch.

