\subsection{Control Strategy}

\subsubsection{Driving Method of MR Clutch}

When the external excitation current or magnetic field is removed, the alignment of magnetic particles in the MRG gradually returns to its initial random state. However, this transition is not instantaneous, the MRG retains elevated viscosity or stiffness for a period, and the locking torque decreases progressively until the zero-damping condition is restored. This behavior exhibits magnetic hysteresis.

To minimize the influence of the hysteresis effect, a demagnetizing current is introduced. This approach facilitates a faster transition of the MR bearing from a high-damping to a zero-damping state. The alternating magnetic field generated by the demagnetizing current is a widely adopted demagnetization technique, which involves periodic reversals of the magnetic field direction. In this study, a sinusoidal signal with decaying amplitude is employed to generate the alternating field, enabling a gradual reduction in magnetic flux density. During this process, the magnetic particles progressively lose their ordered alignment, thereby achieving effective demagnetization. The workflow of the MR clutch driving module is illustrated in Fig. 3.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/F3.pdf}
    \caption{Flow diagram of MR clutch driving system.}
\end{figure}
In this work, a hardware circuit for driving  MR clutches is developed based on the VNH7040AY driver chip. The MCU, integrated into the exoskeleton system, first acquires force data from a sensor mounted at the end of the robotic arm actuator. This data is subsequently mapped to determine the magnitude of the excitation current applied to the MR clutch coil. Control signals derived from this mapping are transmitted to the driver circuit via functional module. To generate excitation current, the MCU delivers control signals that maintain a fixed output direction from the VNH7040AY, resulting in a single-pulse square-wave current across the coil. For demagnetization, the MCU modulates the control signals to trigger high-frequency switching of the output direction, thereby generating an attenuated sinusoidal waveform across the coil to achieve effective demagnetization.When the excitation current applied to the MR clutch reaches 1.3 A, the driver board imposes a threshold limit to prevent further increase in current, thereby avoiding excessive load on the operator. Additionally, a manual demagnetization button is provided to release the MR clutch in case of potential exoskeleton lock-up situations.
Power for the entire driver module is supplied by a lithium polymer (LiPo) battery in conjunction with a step-down converter. The printed circuit board (PCB) is designed to integrate the MCU, driver chip, and power supply module. These components are housed within 3D-printed enclosures, which are ergonomically designed to be worn on the lower back using elastic straps. The structural layout and wearing configuration are illustrated in Supplementary Figure S2.

\subsubsection{Master-Slave Following Control}

Based on the MR clutch design described in this paper, a single-arm upper-limb exoskeleton is developed, as shown in Fig. 4(a). The exoskeleton has four DOFs. To facilitate the study of the positional relationship between the wrist joint's coordinate system and the base coordinate system of the shoulder joint\cite{Cheng2024}, the IMU located at the wrist joint is treated as a virtual 5th rotational joint, with its axis of rotation aligned with the elbow joint. Therefore, the upper-limb exoskeleton can be considered as a five-DOF four-bar linkage model. During the upper-limb motion, it is assumed that the shoulder joint remains stationary relative to the human body. Hence, the joint numbering from the shoulder joint to the wrist joint is defined as 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5. The four-DOF five-bar linkage D-H model established in this paper is shown in Fig. 4(b), with the corresponding D-H parameters provided in Table II.
\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/F4.pdf}
    \caption{(a) Single-arm upper limb exoskeleton based on MR clutches. (b) D-H model of the exoskeleton. (c) Comparative visualization of 3D master-slave following trajectories (see supplementary Video S1). (d) 3D tracking error in Fig. 4(c).}
\end{figure}

The lengths of the four links are denoted as \( L_{1} - L_{4} \), while the joint angles \( \theta_1 - \theta_5 \) correspond to the angular encoder data for joints 1 to 4, and the rotational angle of the virtual 5th joint. The value of \( \theta_5 \) does not influence the final result. The general form of the transformation matrix in the D-H convention is given by:
\begin{equation}
    \label{equation_2}
    {}^{i-1}_{i}T = 
    \begin{bmatrix}
    R & P \\
    0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}

Here, \( R \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3} \) is the rotation matrix that represents the relative orientation of the \( i \)-th frame with respect to the \( (i-1) \)-th frame.
Similarly, \( P \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 1} \) is the translation vector, indicating the displacement from the origin of frame \( i-1 \) to frame \( i \), expressed in the \( (i-1) \)-th coordinate system.

The rotation matrix \( R \) is given by:
\begin{equation}
    \label{equation_3}
    R = 
    \begin{bmatrix}
    \cos\theta_i & -\sin\theta_i & 0 \\
    \sin\theta_i \cos\alpha_{i-1} & \cos\theta_i \cos\alpha_{i-1} & -\sin\alpha_{i-1}  \\
    \sin\theta_i \sin\alpha_{i-1} & \cos\theta_i \sin\alpha_{i-1} & \cos\alpha_{i-1}  \\
    \end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}

The translation vector \( P \) is given by:
\begin{equation}
    \label{equation_4}
    P = 
    \begin{bmatrix}
    a_{i-1} \\
    -\sin\alpha_{i-1} d_i \\
    \cos\alpha_{i-1} d_i \\
    \end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}

By substituting the D-H parameters, the resulting homogeneous transformation matrix is obtained as:
\begin{equation}
\label{equation_5}
{}^{0}T_{5} = {}^{0}T_{1} \cdot {}^{1}T_{2} \cdot {}^{2}T_{3} \cdot {}^{3}T_{4} \cdot {}^{4}T_{5}
\end{equation}

The position of Joint 5's coordinate frame relative to the base frame at the shoulder can be obtained from matrix ${}^{0}T_{5}$. The Euler angles directly obtained from the IMU are used as the end-effector pose information. In addition, a proportional mapping is established based on the working range correspondence between the exoskeleton and the K1 robotic arm. Finally, the control of each joint is achieved using the built-in inverse kinematics solver of the K1 robotic arm.

