\section{Experiments}
\subsection{Teleoperation Experiment}
The effectiveness of the proposed method is evaluated through the follow-up motion demonstrated in Supplementary Video S1. The motion trajectories of the end-effector of the master-side exoskeleton and the slave-side K1 robotic arm are shown in Fig. 4(c). As illustrated in Fig. 4(d), the tracking errors of K1 along the X, Y, and Z axes are well constrained—remaining below 4 mm on the X-axis and under 3 mm on both the Y and Z axes. These results demonstrate that the proposed method enables accurate trajectory reproduction.  Supplementary Videos S2 and S3 further present tracking performance across several basic motion patterns.

\subsection{Force Feedback Experiment}
In the experimental section, surface electromyography (sEMG) signals were utilized to evaluate the effectiveness of MR clutches in delivering force feedback to the teleoperation operator. Compared to conventional force and torque measurements, sEMG data offer a more intuitive reflection of the operator's active muscle exertion when overcoming the locking torque generated by the MR clutches.
Prior to the main experiments, a calibration procedure was conducted to evaluate subject's sEMG signal levels under different locking torques. An sEMG sensor was placed on the biceps brachii (see Supplementary Figure S3), and the subject was instructed to flex the elbow joint against varying levels of locking torque. The relationship between the sEMG signal and the applied torque was established by averaging the results over multiple trials, as shown in Fig. 5(a).
Due to the need to counteract both the exoskeleton's own weight and the idle resistance of the elbow MR clutch, the sEMG signal exhibited a baseline value of approximately 37\,$\mu$V even when the torque was zero. As the torque increased, the sEMG signal increased nearly linearly, indicating a proportional rise in muscle activation.

In high-precision teleoperated robotic arm operations for lunar surface sampling, a force feedback gain strategy is employed to enhance the operator's perception of interaction forces from the slave side. Specifically, the output torque of the MR clutches is scaled to amplify subtle force cues during teleoperation. In the subsequent experiments, the excitation current was adjusted according to Fig. 2(f), such that the output torque of the MR clutches was amplified to five times the actual torque acting on the end-effector. This approach effectively improves force sensitivity and enables the operator to distinguish contact events with greater clarity.

\subsubsection{Obstacle Avoidance}
An experiment was designed to test the force feedback effect of the MR clutch on the operator. A typical scenario for a lunar surface sampling task was created in a simulated lunar work environment. Fig. 5(b) shows the details of the simulated scene, in which the master is the operator wearing the upper-limb exoskeleton, and the slave is the K1 robotic arm mounted on a quadruped robot. A force sensor is installed on the end effector of K1 to sense the force information of K1. First, the quadruped robot brings the K1 robotic arm to the placement area. Then, the operator remotely controls the K1 robotic arm via visual information to grasp the target object. Finally, the operator plans the path for placing the object based on the visual information.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/F5.pdf}
    \caption{Teleoperated exoskeleton for simulated lunar surface sampling tasks. (a) Relationship between the sEMG signals of the biceps brachii and the locking torque of the elbow joint. (b) The operator wore an upper-limb exoskeleton embedded with a sEMG sensor to capture upper muscle activation signals. Meanwhile, the slave K1 robotic arm, transported to the designated sampling zone by a quadruped robot, performed the sampling tasks via teleoperation. During the operation, the MR clutches delivered real-time force feedback to the operator. (c) K1 End-Effector trajectory (see Supplementary Video S4). (d) Comparative analysis between the torque data and the sEMG signals in Supplementary Video S4.}
\end{figure}

However, in actual lunar surface sampling scenarios, visual information may be incomplete due to occlusion by obstacles. Such incomplete visual feedback can lead to collisions with the environment during the grasping and releasing operations. In these situations, force feedback from the MR clutches integrated into the upper-limb joints can be utilized to detect the occurrence and magnitude of collisions. Based on this feedback, the operator can accordingly re-plan the motion trajectory to successfully complete the grasping and releasing tasks.

Supplementary Video S4 presents a demonstration corresponding to the aforementioned task. During the planning of the object release path, the operator encountered two collisions with obstacles. The operator then replanned the trajectory and successfully completed the task by releasing the target into the placement area.
Fig. 5(c) depicts the trajectory of the K1 end-effector from object grasping to release. 
The torque and sEMG data recorded during the two collision events are shown in Fig. 5(d). The first collision produced an root mean square (RMS) torque of approximately 5.3\,N$\cdot$m, with a corresponding RMS sEMG value of about 138\,$\mu$V. The second collision generated an RMS torque of around 4.2\,N$\cdot$m, with a corresponding RMS sEMG value of approximately 129\,$\mu$V.

According to the reference curve in Fig. 5(a), the expected sEMG values under ideal conditions would be 141\,$\mu$V and 118\,$\mu$V, respectively.
Considering the muscle fatigue and psychological factors caused by prolonged wearing of the exoskeleton, we regard fluctuations within 20\,$\mu$V in the sEMG signal as normal\cite{Johnson2017}.
These results indicate that the operator was able to perceive the amplified force feedback and successfully controlled the K1 robotic arm to avoid obstacles and complete the sampling task.

\subsubsection{Stiffness Recognition}
To demonstrate the capability of force feedback in distinguishing objects with different stiffness levels, a second experiment was conducted on the same experimental platform, in which the grasping motion of the K1 robotic arm was replaced by a collision-based motion. The collided objects were classified into three categories according to their stiffness characteristics: the first category featured high-stiffness objects represented by stones, the second category included medium-stiffness objects such as foam plastic, and the third category comprised low-stiffness materials exemplified by gravel. The same protocol was employed to record torque and sEMG signals. Detailed procedures are illustrated in Supplementary Videos S5, S6, and S7.
\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=1]{images/F6.pdf}
    \caption{Comparative analysis of torque and sEMG signals during collisions with objects of varied stiffness. (a) High-stiffness object (see Supplementary Video S5). (b) Medium-stiffness object (see Supplementary Video S6). (c) Low-stiffness object (see Supplementary Video S7).}
\end{figure}

As shown in Fig. 6(a), when colliding with a high-stiffness stone block, the RMS torque from the three collisions had an average value of 8.8\,N$\cdot$m. At this point, the MR clutch maintained a high locking torque. To overcome the resistance and unlock the MR clutch, the operator was required to exert a considerable amount of force. The corresponding RMS value of the sEMG signal exhibited a mean of approximately 215\,$\mu$V. Given the high stiffness of the object, applying a larger force to overcome the MR clutch's torque and control the K1's movement would increase the compression between the force sensor and the stone block. As a result, the increased excitation current enhances the magnetic field, generating higher locking torque in the MR clutch. This feedback mechanism further limits joint motion, providing the operator with stronger and more perceptible force feedback.

Fig. 6(b) illustrates the collision between manipulator K1 and a medium-stiffness elastic material. A total of four collisions occurred, with the average value of the torque RMS from these collisions being 7.1\,N$\cdot$m. The corresponding average RMS value of the sEMG signal was approximately 177\,$\mu$V.
Fig. 6(c) presents the results of collisions between K1 and a low-stiffness granular material. Similarly, four collisions were recorded, with the average value of the torque RMS being approximately 2.8\,N$\cdot$m, and the average RMS value of the sEMG signal around 102\,$\mu$V.

According to the calibration curve in Fig. 5(a), the ideal sEMG values for the conditions in Fig. 6(a), Fig. 6(b) and Fig. 6(c) are 228\,$\mu$V, 185\,$\mu$V and 86\,$\mu$V, respectively, which basically aligns with the previously mentioned 20\,$\mu$V margin of error.
These collision tests involving objects of varying stiffness demonstrate that the MR clutch can effectively simulate damping forces generated upon contact. Therefore, employing MR clutches as force feedback actuators in exoskeleton systems proves to be a feasible and effective solution.

\section{Conclusion} 
We propose a semi-active single-arm exoskeleton based on MR clutches to enable high-precision force feedback in robotic arm teleoperation. The MR clutch was structurally designed, magnetically simulated, and experimentally evaluated, achieving a maximum locking torque of 42\,N$\cdot$m and a torque-to-mass ratio of 93.6\,N$\cdot$m/kg—a value approximately 246\% higher than that of a representative prior design\cite{Pisetskiy2021}-balancing lightweight design with a wide force feedback range. A dedicated driving method and master-slave tracking control strategy were also developed. Experimental validation through obstacle avoidance and stiffness recognition tasks demonstrated that the MR clutch-based force feedback not only detects collisions but also distinguishes different contact intensities. These results confirm the feasibility of the proposed approach in practical teleoperation scenarios. Future work will focus on enhancing system responsiveness and robustness, and exploring adaptive algorithms to further improve force perception accuracy under dynamic conditions.

