\subsection{Datasets}

An overview of the datasets that were used in the literature for text/query-driven video summarization is provided in Table \ref{tab:datasets}. The earliest used ones by Sharghi et al. in \cite{10.1007/978-3-319-46484-8_1}, were the UT Egocentric (UTE) \cite{6247820} and the TV Episodes (TVE) \cite{Yeung2014VideoSETVS} datasets. UTE contains four egocentric videos $3-5$ hours long that were recorded in an uncontrolled environment from a first-person view wearable camera. TV Episodes includes four videos $45$ min. long that were captured from a third person's viewpoint. The videos of the UTE and TV Episodes have been annotated by human subjects in \cite{Yeung2014VideoSETVS} with one-sentence descriptions at the level of short video fragments that last $5$ and $10$ sec., respectively. Three reference text-based summaries were defined per video as a subset of the textual annotations, and used for evaluating the output of the query-driven summarization method based on the ROUGE-SU metric \cite{lin-2004-rouge}. The UTE dataset was further extended by Sharghi et al. in \cite{8099712}, by adding human-annotated video summaries for $46$ user queries that were defined using two or three concepts per video, forming the Query-Focused Video Summarization (QFVS) dataset. Vasudevan et al. \cite{10.1145/3123266.3123297} introduced the RAD dataset which contains $190$ videos that were retrieved from YouTube using different queries and annotated with query relevance labels at the frame and fragment level. Labels range from 0 (Bad) to 3 (Very Good), where ``Bad'' indicates that the frame is both irrelevant and of low aesthetic quality (e.g. blurred, bad contrast, etc.). This dataset was extended by Huang et al. in \cite{10.1145/3372278.3390695}, by computing relevance scores for each query-video pair of the dataset. The SumMe \cite{10.1007/978-3-319-10584-0_33} and TVSum \cite{7299154} benchmarking datasets for generic video summarization were extended by Wei et al. \cite{10.5555/3504035.3504062} with fragment-level descriptions of the visual content, to train a video summarization method that takes into account the video semantics. Finally, Cizmeciler et al. \cite{10.1007/s11042-022-12442-w} introduced the Activity Related Summaries (ARS) dataset that contains $10$ YouTube videos of $2$-$12$ min. duration. These videos were annotated by humans at the shot level using a pre-defined list of $60$ attributes, and $10$ ground-truth summaries were defined for each video using one/two-word queries from a list of $142$ queries.

As shown in Table \ref{tab:datasets}, contrary to the existing datasets, the extended S-VideoXum dataset for script-driven video summarization: i) contains a remarkably large number of videos from different domains with diverse visual content, ii) provides $10$ different ground-truth summaries per video, thus allowing to train a summarization method to produce different summaries for a given video based on the users' preferences about the content of each summary, and iii) is divided in train, validation and test sets, enabling the selection of a well-trained model that generalizes well to unseen data.  

\section{Problem Statement and Dataset}

\subsection{Problem formulation}

Let us assume an input video of $N$ frames and a user-provided script (a free-form text description) of $M$ sentences outlining the content of the desired video summary; different sentences of the script may refer to different parts of the full-length video with varying visual and semantic content. Then, script-driven video summarization aims to select a subset of frames or fragments of the video that are both relevant to one or more sentences of the user-provided script, and important for an overall understanding of the full-length video. The selected frames/fragments should form a video summary that does not exceed a predefined length $L$. Following the paradigm in the video summarization literature \cite{9594911}, $L$ is typically set to $15\%$ of the full-length video duration. 

Another task that aims to spot parts of the video that are relevant to a freeform query is the task of temporal sentence grounding in videos \cite{10.1145/3532626} (a.k.a. video grounding). Nevertheless, this task tries to localize one target segment from the full-length video, that corresponds to a given sentence query. On the contrary, script-driven video summarization aims to generate a concise summary, and in this process it needs to select multiple video fragments that relate to the complex query, as well as other less relevant parts that are necessary for providing a synopsis of the full-length video.

\subsection{Script-driven video summarization dataset}
\label{subsection:sdvs}

To overcome observed weaknesses of small video summarization datasets, related to the sensitivity of the evaluation outcomes to different data splits (as shown in \cite{10.1145/3394171.3413632}) and the inability to select a well-trained model in the absence of a validation set (discussed in \cite{9594911}), we focused on the VideoXum large-scale dataset for cross-modal video summarization \cite{10334011}. VideoXum includes $14,000$ open-domain videos up to $12.5$ min. long with diverse visual content, from the ActivityNet Captions dataset \cite{8237345}. Each video is associated with multiple ground-truth video summaries - in the form of frame-level binary scores which denote the inclusion (label ``1'') or not (label ``0'') of a frame in the video summary - that were obtained by $10$ different human annotators. The dense video captions from the ActivityNet Captions dataset that are considered as text summaries in \cite{10334011} provide a high-level description of the full-length video and thus are more appropriate for multimodal generic video summarization purposes. The existence of multiple ground-truth summaries per video ($10$ in total) makes the VideoXum dataset well-suited for the script-driven video summarization task, as it allows to train a summarization method to generate different summaries for a given video, driven by the provided script about the content of each summary. Furthermore, its significantly large size allows to split the available videos into training, validation and test set. Such a data split supports the extensive training of a model using a very wide set of training samples, the selection of a well-trained model that exhibits good generalization capacity through the use of an adequately big validation set, and the robust evaluation of its performance using a large number of testing samples.

To make VideoXum suitable for training and evaluation of script-driven video summarization methods, we extended it by producing natural language descriptions of the different ground-truth summaries that are available per video. For this, we employed the publicly-available\footnote{https://huggingface.co/llava-hf/LLaVA-NeXT-Video-7B-hf} state-of-the-art Large Multimodal Model LLaVA-NeXT-7B \cite{li2024llavanext-strong}. To align with the adopted video representation strategy in \cite{10334011}, we processed each ground-truth video summary using one frame per second and obtained the natural language descriptions using the following prompt: ``Describe the important scenes in the video''. The maximum number of generated tokens was set equal to $200$, and a $4$-bit quantization was applied to reduce computational cost. Moreover, to allow performance comparisons with multimodal approaches for generic video summarization (which are compatible with our method in terms of the used data modalities), we produced natural language descriptions of the full-length videos, as well. Once again, we sampled these videos keeping one frame per second and generated descriptions using the LLaVA-NEXT-7B model and the same prompt. 

