The proposed SD-VSum network architecture was compared with CLIP-It \cite{10.5555/3540261.3541333} that exhibits state-of-the-art performance on query-driven and multimodal (generic) video summarization. The results in the upper part of Table \ref{tab:table1} show that SD-VSum performs clearly better than CLIP-It on the extended S-VideoXum dataset, achieving an F-Score that is almost $2\%$ higher than the F-Score of CLIP-It. Since SD-VSum and CLIP-It are similar in terms of network architecture (as discussed in Section \ref{subsec:network}), we tried to spot what causes this noticeable difference in the script-driven summarization performance of these architectures. For this, we conducted an ablation study that involved variants of SD-VSum that resemble the network architecture of CLIP-It, as well as a variant that focuses on the use of a scaling process when computing the attention weights. In particular, we considered the following variants:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Variant \#1 applies the same single vector representation approach for the user script, with CLIP-It. 
    \item Variant \#2 extends the previous variant by containing the same number of attention heads with CLIP-It. 
    \item Variant \#3 extends the previous variant by performing a scaling process when computing the attention weights (by dividing with $\sqrt{D}$, where $D$ is the size of the used embeddings), simulating further the language-guided attention mechanism of CLIP-It.
    \item Variant \#4 uses multiple embeddings for the user script and maintains the same number of attention heads ($8$), but performs the aforementioned scaling process when computing the attention weights.
\end{itemize}
The findings of our ablation study are reported in the lower part of Table \ref{tab:table1}. The performance of Variant \#1 highlights the impact of producing and using a significantly more condensed representation for the user script, which leads to a noticeable drop in the script-driven video summarization performance (almost $2\%$ lower than SD-VSum). The performance of Variant \#2 indicates the slight effect of the scaling process when using a single vector representation for the user script ($0.1\%$ lower than the performance of Variant \#1), an effect that is more pronounced when the script is represented using multiple embeddings (as denoted by the performance of Variant \#4 which is $0.6\%$ lower than that of SD-VSum). Finally, the reduction of the number of employed attention heads leads to further performance reduction ($0.5\%$ lower than the performance of Variant \#2). In general, the observed performance for the CLIP-It method and the variants of SD-VSum that utilize the same approach for representing the user script, documents the contribution of using a more coarse-grained sentence-level representation of the user script and points out the effectiveness of the integrated cross-modal attention mechanism in the SD-VSum network architecture.

