\subsection{Operational Semantics}\label{ss:os}
We endow our quantum language with a call-by-value operational semantics,
given in terms of the (deterministic) PARS
\[
  \cdot \lra{\cdot} \cdot \subseteq \cTerms \times \Rpos \times  \Distr(\cTerms)
\]
on ground terms, see Figure~\ref{tab:TRS}.
We briefly comment on the non-classical constructs of the language.

Let
$\ext{U}_n$ denote the extension of a unitary $U$ to a unitary of length $n \in \mathbb{N}$
and, by convention, the identity when $n \leq l(U)$.
Formally, $\ext{U}_n \defsym U \otimes I_{n - l(U)}$ for $l(U) \leq n$, and otherwise $\ext{U}_n \defsym I_n$.
We write $\ext{U}$ for $\ext{U}_{l(\ket{\psi})}$, when applied to a quantum state $\ket{\psi}$.
The semantics of quantum gate application is defined using this extension.

The measurement rule for $\meas$ is the only probabilistic rule, and reduces to a probability distribution consisting of (at most) two terms, corresponding to the two possible measurement outcomes.
Measurement is designed to behave as a sampling operator for our soundness proof. This is the reason why these two injections are used as marks of a measurement outcome.
Here, the probabilities $p_i \ket{\psi}$ and the post-collapse quantum states $M_i \ket{\psi}$, for $i \in \{0,1\}$ are defined as usual:
\begin{align*}
  p_i  \ket{\psi} &\triangleq  tr(\ext{\ketbra{i}} \ketbra{\psi} )
  &
    M_i \ket{\psi} & \triangleq
                     \begin{cases}
                       \frac{\ext{\ketbra{i}}}{\sqrt{p_i \ket  \psi}} \ket{\psi}& \text{if } p_i \ket \psi\neq 0 \\
                       \ket{\psi}   & \text{if } p_i \ket \psi= 0.
                     \end{cases}
\end{align*}
For the sake of having a total definition, we have defined $M_i$ as an identity, when $p_i \ket \psi = 0$.
Note however that in this case $M_i \ket{\psi} = \ket{\psi}$ will not occur in the support of the reduct distribution, as it will be attributed probability $0$.

\noindent The tick rule is the only rule that accounts for a cost. Finally, the last inference closes
the reduction relation under \emph{evaluation contexts}

  \begin{tblr}{colspec={rl},rowsep=1pt,columns={leftsep=1pt,rightsep=1pt,mode=math}}
    E \bnfdef& [] \bnfmid E \app \sv \bnfmid \st \app E \bnfmid  \unary{E}  \bnfmid \meas{E}\bnfmid  E \qtimes \sv    \bnfmid \st \qtimes E  \bnfmid  \cons(\vec{\st}_0;\vec{\st}_1,E,\vec{\sv}_1) 
    \bnfmid   \cons(\vec{\st}_0,E,\vec{\sv}_0;\vec{\sv}_1) \bnfmid \caseof{E}{\st_0}{\st_1}
  \end{tblr}

We denote by $\disto{}$ the lifting of $\lra{}$ to distributions $\Distr(\cTerms)$.

\subsection{Type System}\label{ss:ts}
\subsubsection{Basic types.}
The set of \emph{basic types} $\Basic=\{\typ , \typ_1,\ldots \}$ is defined as the disjoint union of the set of \emph{quantum basic types} $\Qbasic=\{\qtyp,\qtyp^1,\ldots \}$ and the set of \emph{classical basic types} $\Cbasic=\{\ctyp,\ctyp^1,\ldots \}$.
Each type $\typ \in \Basic$ comes with a finite set of constructor symbols $\Cons(\typ)$, each constructor symbol in $\Cons(\typ)$ having a fixed type signature of the shape $\cons :: \ctyp^1 \times \ldots \times \ctyp^n \times \qtyp^1 \times \ldots \times \qtyp^m \to \typ$, with  $m,n \in \mathbb{N}$.  We will sometimes write $\cons :: \vec \ctyp ;\! \vec\qtyp \to \typ$ as a shorthand notation, and also we may write just $\typ$ when $n=m=0$.


For each $\ctyp \in \Cbasic$, the constructor symbols in $\Cons(\ctyp)$ cannot take quantum basic types in their signature, i.e., $m=0$. Hence each $\cons$ in $\Cons(\ctyp)$ has a signature $\cons :: \vec \ctyp;\! \to \ctyp$. The type $\Nat$ of numerals such that $\Cons(\Nat) \triangleq \{\suc,\zero\}$, with $\suc:: \Nat; \to \Nat$ and $\zero::\Nat$ is an example of type in $\Cbasic$.

The set $ \Qbasic$ includes the type  $\qbty$ of quantum states. The type $\qbty$ has no constructor, i.e.,  $\Cons(\qbty)=\emptyset$.  The type of measurement outcomes $\Out\in  \Qbasic$ contains the two injections $\Cons(\Out)\triangleq \{\inj_0,\inj_1\}$ of type signature $\inj_i ::\,; \qbty \to \Out$.
The type $\Qlist$ of quantum lists can be defined $\Cons(\Qlist) \triangleq \{\qnil, \qcons \} $, with $\qnil:: \Qlist$ and $\qcons::\,;\qbty \times \Qlist \to \Qlist$.

%RP: yes needed
For notational convenience, we will sometimes extend destructors to each data type in $\typ \in \Basic$ to simulate an exhaustive pattern matching. For example,
$\mathsf{case}\,\st\,\mathsf{of}\,\{ \zero \mapsto \st_0\mid  \suc(x;\!) \mapsto \st_1  \}$ will be syntactic sugar for $\mathsf{case}\,\st\,\mathsf{of}\,\{\zero \mapsto \st_0\mid z \mapsto  \mathsf{case}\,\st\,\mathsf{of}\,\{\suc(x;\!) \mapsto \st_1\mid y \mapsto  \st_2 \} \}$, for some arbitrary term $\st_2$.




\subsubsection{Types.}
The set of  \emph{duplicable types} $\CTypes$ contains all types $\cty$, that can be cloned, and is defined in Figure~\ref{fig:TS} as the set of types that are either  classical basic types or functional types. There are two distinct arrow types for abstractions $\ty \multimap \ty'$ and $\cty \Rightarrow \ty$, depending on whether the function takes affine data (including quantum data) of type $\ty$ as input or exponential data (only duplicable data) of type $\cty$ as input. %Quantum inputs will be treated in an affine way (they can be used at most once) whereas no restriction is put on classical data.
The set of types $\Types$ extends strictly $\CTypes$ with quantum basic types.

The type discipline of Figure~\ref{fig:TS} is presented in the form of a dual intuitionistic type system à la Barber and Plotkin~\cite{BP96}.  Typing judgments have the shape
    $\cctx;\qctx \vdash \st : \ty$, for some term $\st \in \Terms$ and some type $\ty$.    Given a sequence of affine contexts $\vec \qctx=\qctx_1,\ldots,\qctx_n$, a sequence of terms $\vec \st=\st_1,\ldots,\st_n$, and a sequence of types $\vec \typ = \typ_1,\ldots,\typ_m$, we write $\cctx;\vec \qctx \vdash \vec \st : \vec\ty$ when $m=n$ and $\cctx; \qctx_i \vdash \st_i : \ty_i$ hold for all $i \leq n$.
  The typing environment $\Gamma;\Delta$ is divided into an \emph{exponential typing context} $\Gamma$ and an \emph{affine typing context} $\Delta$.  The exponential context deals with all the clonable data, including the type $\ctyp$ for classical basic data.
The affine context aims at ensuring that variables of type $\qtyp$, corresponding to quantum data, can be used at most once, hence forbidding their cloning.
Notice that this allows discarding variables of type $\qtyp$. Since the quantum data is given within the terms and there is no construction allowing a variable to refer to a part of a quantum state, a variable of type $\qtyp$ cannot be entangled with another state and can thus be safely discarded.
The outcome of a measurement is typed by the type $\Out\in\Qbasic$ to emphasize that measuring a quantum state results in a classical outcome together with a post-collapse quantum state (hence non-clonable).
The  rule $(\Rightarrow_e)$ for classical implication and the rule $(\mathsf{rec})$ for recursion  require the affine context to be empty to avoid cloning quantum data during the reduction.



From now on, we will restrict our study to typable terms and, by a slight abuse of language, we will consider that $\cTerms$ denotes the set of typable closed terms throughout the paper.


\subsection{Design choices}
A central design choice in our language is to keep the syntax simple and avoid cumbersome notation. For this reason, we do not include pattern matching on quantum data, and certain constructions such as the tensor product of functions are not expressible in the current setting. This is a deliberate trade-off: the language is conceived as a bridge between quantum and probabilistic programming, with a focus on reasoning about resource usage rather than the evolution of quantum states. In particular, while sequential composition of circuits is supported, parallel composition (tensor product) is not. This implies that circuits must be constructed with a fixed size and structure. Despite these restrictions, the language remains universal in the sense that it can express any quantum circuit. In addition, we adopt a denotational semantics that, instead of relying on standard representations such as density matrices or completely positive maps, is oriented towards cost analysis, capturing the structural and quantitative aspects of computation. These choices support the goals of the language and provide a solid foundation for future extensions to incorporate more conventional quantum constructs, such as pattern matching.



\section{Cost-Structure Transformers}


Following \cite{ABL21,AMPPZ22}, we will now define the expectation transformer, mapping
terms of our quantum language to \emph{cost-structure terms}.
Conceptually, the transformer is an adaptation of the one-pass call-by-value CPS transformation by Danvy and Nielsen~\cite{DanvyN03}.
In this transformation, the continuation parameter should be understood as a function
giving the expected cost or value of the continuation. 
Cost-structure terms will be defined in a generic way and by specifying the expectation operators we will delineate both cases for expected cost and value.

\subsection{The Cost-Structure Language}
As a first step, we define the \emph{cost-structure language}, an intermediate language designed for expectation analysis with expressions denoting costs or values. This intermediate language is a symbolic language for reasoning abstractly about properties of quantum programs rather than a quantum programming language, as, by default, it permits quantum data duplication and includes any real number. 

The set $\TTerms$ of \emph{cost-structure terms} and the set $\TValues$ of \emph{cost-structure values} are defined by the grammar of \Cref{fig:cost:Grammar}, where $\tx, \tf $ belong to a fixed countable set $\VAR$ of variables. 
Again, $\mathsf{fv}(\te)$ denotes the free variables of $\te$.
As terms of the cost-structure language are in essence results of a CPS translation, redexes will occur only in head position.  This is reflected in the syntax and can be viewed as a kind of  A-normal form~\cite{SF92} where only trivial arguments serve as operands of function application.

The cost-structure language still includes most of the constructs of the quantum language of Figure~\ref{fig:Grammar}: the standard constructs of the lambda calculus, the constructs for quantum data,  and the constructs for basic data (there is no more a distinction between quantum and classical data using a semi-column) and recursion. 
However, a new construct $\Meas{b}$ ($b \in \{0,1\})$ for the post-collapse quantum state and  \emph{cost-structure operators} (\emph{CS-ops}, for short) have been substituted to the measurement construct and the tick construct. The CS-ops build terms that represent cost-structure operations (see Section~\ref{ss:k}), but do not represent quantum programs: the (non-negative) real constants $\breal{r}$ ($r \in \Rpos$), $\cadd$ for adding costs in a cost-structure, $\cbary{{\cdot}}$ for computing barycentric sums in a Kegelspitze.

