\subsubsection{Interpretation of terms and their properties}
Let the \emph{valuation} $\rho$ be a partial map from the set of variables $\VAR$ to the set $\cup_{\tty \in  \TTypes} \typesem{\tty}$. A valuation $\rho$ is \emph{valid} for a CS context $\tctx$, denoted by $\rho \Vdash\tctx$, if for all $\tx : \tty \in \tctx$, we have $\rho(\tx) \in \typesem{\tty}$. Let $\emptyv$ be the defined nowhere valuation and $\rho\{\tx := \tv\}$ be the valuation extending $\rho$ by $\rho\{\tx := \tv\}(\tx)=\tv$. Let also $\rho\{\vec{\tx} := \vec{\tv}\}$ be the natural extension to sequences of the same length.

The denotational semantics of terms with respect to a cost-structure $\kost$ and a valuation $\rho$ is given in \Cref{fig:denot}.
The semantics is defined on typing judgments but to avoid cumbersome notations, it is only defined on terms.
That is, giving a typing judgment $ \tctx \INF \te : \tty$, the interpretation $\ksem{\env}{\kost}{\te}$ denotes an element of  $\typesem{\tty}$, for some  valuation $\rho$ such that $\rho \Vdash\tctx$ (see Theorem~\ref{thm:sound}).

In Figure~\ref{fig:denot}, $p_0 \ksem{\env}{\kost}{\tv}$ denotes the probability  of measuring the outcome $0$ on the first qubit of the quantum state $\ksem{\env}{\kost}{\tv}$ ($p_0$ is defined in Section~\ref{ss:os}) and $\ksem{\env}{\kost}{\Meas{b}{\tv}}$ denotes the collapse of the quantum state $\tv$ after a measurement with outcome $b \in \{0,1\}$. For example, for $\ket \psi = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt3} \ket{00} + \tfrac{1}{\sqrt3} \ket{10} + \tfrac{1}{\sqrt3} \ket{11}$, $p_1 \ket \psi$ evaluates to $\tfrac{2}{3}$ and $\ksem{\env}{\kost}{\Meas{1}{\ket \psi}}$ evaluates to  $\tfrac1{\sqrt{2}}\ket{10}+\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\ket{11}$ (the normalized state obtained after measuring the outcome $1$).


\begin{theorem}\label{thm:sound}
If $\tctx \INF \te : \tty$ then
$\ksem{\env}{\kost}{\te } \in \typesem{\tty}$ for all $\env \Vdash \tctx$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
  By induction on the typing derivation $\tctx \INF \te : \tty$. 
\end{proof}

\subsection{Quantum Expectation Transformers}




For a fixed cost-structure type $\kty$, we define two transformations $\qetv{(\cdot)}$ and $\qet{\cdot}{\cdot}$ from the quantum language to the cost-structure language in \Cref{fig:ect}. 
Towards that end, we assume a one-to-one correspondence between variables $\sx \in \Var$ and variables $\tx \in \VAR$,  through the use of lower/upper case letters for variables.
The value transformation $\qetv{(\cdot)}$ maps a value in $\Values$ to a value
in $\TValues$ and  the term transformation $\qet{\cdot}{\cdot}$  maps a term in $\Terms$ and a continuation in $ \Costfuns$ to a term in $\TTerms$.


Let $\cdot$ denote the empty sequence. In~\Cref{fig:ect}, these two transformations are also extended to sequences of terms and values:
\begin{align*}
 \qetv{(\cdot)} &\defsym \cdot & \qetv{(\sv,\vec \sv)} &\defsym  \qetv{\sv},\qetv{\vec \sv}\\
\qet{{ \cdot}}{\cont} &\defsym \cont &  \qet{{\vec \st,\st }}{\cont} &\defsym \qet{\st}{\lambda \tx. \qet{{\vec \st\,}}{\cont \app \tx}}
 \end{align*}
   In the transformation of a constructor application (see~\Cref{fig:ect}),  $\lambda \vec \tx_i$ denotes a sequence of lambda abstractions whose length matches the length of $\vec \st_i$. In the particular case where $\vec \st_i$ is empty (i.e., equal to $\cdot$), we set $\lambda \vec \tx_i.\te \triangleq \te$.
The type translation $\ttpe{(-)}$ is also homomorphically extended to typing contexts. 


In order to define some relevant resource analyses, we can specialize the $\qetb$ transformation through the cost-structure in which it will be interpreted. To capture costs, we use $(\Rext, +)$, i.e., the real numbers plus infinity with standard addition, as the tick should be counted as an atomic cost of $1$. To compute expected values, the tick shall be forgotten, hence the use of the forgetful addition in a generic cost-structure $(\keg, \fplus)$.



\begin{definition}[Quantum Expectation Transformers]\label{def:qet}
Given a cost-structure $\kost$, the \emph{quantum expectation transformer} $\qe_{\kost}$ is the function in $\cTerms\to (\cTerms\to \kost) \to \kost$ defined by:
\[
\qe_\kost(\st)(k)\triangleq\ksem{\{K:=k\}}{\kost}{\qet{\st}{K}}.
\]
Moreover, taking the cost-structure $\kost$ to be
\begin{itemize}
\item $(\Rext, +)$ yields an expected cost transformer:
\begin{align*}
\qec &:\cTerms\to (\cTerms\to \Rext) \to  \Rext\\
\qec &\triangleq \qe_{(\Rext, +)}
\end{align*}
\item $(\keg, \fplus)$ yields the expected value function:
\begin{align*}
\qev &:\cTerms\to (\cTerms\to \keg) \to \keg\\
\qev &\triangleq \qe_{(\keg, \fplus)}
\end{align*}
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\newcommand{\prpred}{P}



\subsection{Properties of Expectation Transformers}

We now prove that the quantum expectation transformer allows us to compute the expected cost of a quantum program (Theorem~\ref{thm:ecost}) by noting that our quantum language is an instance of the probabilistic calculus of \cite{ABL21},
and then relying on the expectation transformer proven sound in this work.


\begin{theorem}\label{thm:ecost}
For each term $\st \in \cTerms$, it holds that $$\ecost[\disto]{\st} = \qec(\st)(\lambda X.0).$$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Using \cite[Theorem~4.15]{ABL21}, noting that the quantum language is an instance of the probabilistic language of~\cite{ABL21}. Indeed,  the measurement $\meas$ is a sampling operator, whose operational semantics has been designed so as to be step by step equivalent to the operational semantics of this language (including the treatment of the quantum case).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:evalue}
For each term $\st \in \cTerms$ and $f \in \cTerms \to \keg$, it holds that  $$\evalue[\disto]{\st}{f} = {\qev(\st)(f)}.$$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The proof is similar to the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:ecost}, replacing the cost-structure $(\Rext,+)$ by $(\keg, \fplus)$.
\end{proof}


\section{Analysis Through Refinement Types}
Theorems~\ref{thm:ecost} and~\ref{thm:evalue} show how the kind of extensional properties that we
are interested in are expressible as cost-structure terms. This eliminates the need to reason
directly via quantum semantics, and enables classical analysis tools. To show this,
we adapt one such technique, a \emph{refinement type system} \cite{FP91}, to the cost-structure language.
As one practical application, in combination with expectation transformers it allows us
to derive upper-bounds on expected costs and values (Theorems~\ref{cor:ub:ecost} and~\ref{cor:ub:value}).

\subsection{Refinement Types}
Refinement types can be seen as a specific form of dependent type system where
logical formulae are used to constrain base types. Our system, depicted in \Cref{fig:refine:ty} extends upon the simply typed system imposed on the cost-structure language in 3 essential ways:
\begin{varitemize}
  \item base types $\refine{\rtb}{\form}$ permit refining base types $\rtb \in \{\kty,\realty\} \cup \Basic$
  of $CS$ to values $\tz$ satisfying the formula $\form$;
  \item function types $(\tx \ofty \rty) \Rightarrow \rty'$ become dependent, where the term variable $\tx$ of type $\rty$ may occur in $\rty'$;
  \item universal quantification $\forall \tx\! \ofty\! \rty.\ \rty'$ allows the bounded quantification
  of $\tx$, constrained by refinement type $\rty$, within $\rty'$.
\end{varitemize}
For instance, $\Nat^{> 0} = \refine{\Nat}{\tz \neq 0}$ defines the type of strictly positive natural numbers,
or
\[
  (X : \Nat^{> 0}) \Rightarrow (Y : \Nat^{>0}) \Rightarrow \refine{\Nat}{Z \leq X \land Z \leq Y}
\]
refers to a function that takes two strictly positive naturals, and produces as output a natural smaller than
both inputs.

As indicated in \Cref{fig:refine:ty},
formulae $\form$ are first-order formulae over Boolean predicates $P$ (e.g., $P(X_1,X_2,X_3) \defsym X_1 = X_2 \otimes X_3$,
stating that $X_1$ is the result of tensoring $X_2$ and $X_3$).
While we keep the set of predicates open, we do assume that formulae $\form$ follow a simple typing regime, and write
$\tctx \INFb \form : \Bool$ to say that $\form$ is a well-typed formula, with variables taking types
according to the context $\tctx$. This way, we ensure that refinements can always be interpreted as Booleans.
We write $\form[\tv / \tx]$ for the formula obtained by replacing all occurrences of $\tx$ by the cost-structure term $\te$ in $\form$, or simply $\form[\tv]$ when $\tx$ is clear from context. This notation extends to
refinement types. Note however that, in $\refine{\rtb}{\form}$, $(\tx \ofty \rty) \Rightarrow \rty'$
and $\forall \tx : \rty.\ \rty'$
the variables $\tz$ and $\tx$ are bound in $\form$ and in $\rty'$, respectively.
To avoid notational overhead, we write the unrefined type $\refine{\rtb}{\top}$ simply as $\rtb$, and
$(\tx \ofty \rty) \Rightarrow \rty'$ as $\rty \Rightarrow \rty'$ when $\tx$ does not occur free in $\rty'$.


Typing judgments of the refinement type system take the form
$
  \rctx \INFr \te : \rty
$
where $\rctx$ is a refinement type context, $\te$ a term in the cost-structure language, and $\rty$ a refinement type. Context $\rctx$ are lists of variable bindings $\tx : \rty$ and formulae $\form$.
The symbol $\cdot$ is used to denote the empty context. In a context $\rctx, \tx : \rty, \rctx'$,
the variable binding $\tx : \rty$ binds the variable $\tx$ in $\rctx'$ (but not in $\rctx$).
The set of variables occurring free in $\rctx$ are denoted by $\mathsf{fv}(\rctx)$.
Formulae in typing contexts will be used to incorporate a path-based analysis into the typing regime,
they restrict the set of environments $\rho$ that a context will be interpreted as.
For instance, the context
\[
  X : \Nat, Y : \refine{\Nat}{Z \leq X}, X \neq Y
\]
refers to all environments $\rho$ where $\rho(X)$ is strictly larger than $\rho(Y)$.

Not every judgment is well-formed. In $\rctx \INFr \te : \rty$, we require that in $\rctx$ each variable is bound exactly once in a typing
context, and that $\rty$ is closed wrt. $\rctx$. Well-formedness of contexts and types (under a context)
is made precise with the judgments $\INFwf \rctx$ and $\rctx \INFwf \rty$, see \Cref{fig:refine:ty}.
In the rules, $\skel{\rty}$ ($\skel{\rctx}$) refers to simple type (context) obtained by dropping all refinements.
Specifically,
 \begin{align*}
 \skel{ \refine{\rtb}{\form}} &\triangleq \rtb & \skel{(\tx \ofty \rty) \Rightarrow \rty'} &\triangleq \skel{\rty} \Rightarrow \skel{\rty'}  &
  \skel{\forall \tx \ofty \rty.\ \rty'} &\triangleq \skel{\rty'}
 \end{align*}
 We extend this definition to RT contexts in the expected way.
The well-formedness will be necessary to the well-definedness of the interpretation of typing judgments.
From now on, whenever we write a judgment $\rctx \INFr \te : \rty$, we implicitly assume it is well-formed,
i.e., $\rctx \INFwf \rty$.

