\begin{abstract}
% Generative recommendations (GR), consists of item tokenizer and generative Large Language Models (LLMs), are achieving notable successes across numerous scenes. Most existing research efforts focus on inventing powerful item tokenizers or advancing decoding strategies to achieve better results, remaining the LLM fine-tuning strategy for GR unexplored. Current methods typically rely on next-token prediction loss of supervised fine-tuning (SFT) or recommendation-customized direct preference optimization (DPO) strategies. While the former relies on the single positive sample from the offline dataset, risking the overfitting and poor generalization ability. The latter incorporates the exploratory negative samples but still suffer from reward-maximizing towards the single positive sample, usually refer as exposure bias problem. Recent advancements of Generative Flow Networks (GFlowNets) provide a possible solution to explore potential positive samples by attributing the generation probability proportion to the reward for specific items, significantly enhancing the diversity of high-reward results for multi-step reasoning tasks. In this paper, we regard the \textbf{\underline{GR}} as a multi-step generation task and thus construct a \textbf{\underline{GFlow}}Nets-based fine-tuning framework (\textbf{\name}). This framework absorb the collaborative knowledge from the traditional recommender systems to construct the curriculum-style trajectory sampler and multi-faceted reward model. With the property of diverse generation of GFlowNets, together with the sampling and heuristic weighting, \name becomes a promising solution to alleviate the exposure bias problem. Abundant empirical results on two datasets and three GR backbones highlights the effectiveness and robustness of \name. We provide the implementation code in \textbf{Supplementary Material}.

Generative recommendations (GR), which usually include item tokenizers and generative Large Language Models (LLMs), have demonstrated remarkable success across a wide range of scenarios. The majority of existing research efforts primarily concentrate on developing powerful item tokenizers or advancing LLM decoding strategies to attain superior performance. However, the critical fine-tuning step in GR frameworks, which is essential for adapting LLMs to recommendation data, remains largely unexplored. Current approaches predominantly rely on either the next-token prediction loss of supervised fine-tuning (SFT) or recommendation-specific direct preference optimization (DPO) strategies. %The former depends solely on a single positive sample from the collected offline dataset, which poses a risk of overfitting and leads to poor generalization ability. The latter incorporates exploratory negative samples; nevertheless, it still encounters the issue of maximizing rewards towards the single positive sample. 
Both methods ignore the exploration of possible positive unobserved samples, which is commonly referred to as the exposure bias problem.
% Recent progress in Generative Flow Networks (GFlowNets) offers a potential solution. By attributing the generation probability proportion to the reward for specific items, GFlowNets significantly enhance the diversity of high-reward outcomes in compositional generation tasks.
To mitigate this problem, this paper treats the \textbf{\underline{GR}} as a multi-step generation task and constructs a \textbf{\underline{GFlow}}Nets-based fine-tuning framework (\textbf{\name}). The proposed framework integrates collaborative knowledge from traditional recommender systems to create an adaptive trajectory sampler and a comprehensive reward model. Leveraging the diverse generation property of GFlowNets, along with sampling and heuristic weighting techniques, \name emerges as a promising approach to mitigate the exposure bias problem.
Extensive empirical results on two real-world datasets and with two different GR backbones highlight the effectiveness and robustness of \name. %The implementation code is provided in the \textbf{Supplementary Material}.

\end{abstract}




