\begin{abstract}

% Video story interaction enables viewers to directly engage with and actively explore narrative content, gaining personalized experiences. 
% However, existing interaction methods are limited to user selection; the interactive content is limited to specially designed narratives; and the interactive experience lacks user customization. 
% To address this, we propose an interactive system based on user intent. 
% Our system uses Vision Language Model (VLM) to enable machines to understand video stories, and then combines Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and Multi Agent System (MAS) to create multiple characters and scene crossing experiences that grow with the progress of the video story. 
% It includes three stages: 1) \textit{Video story data processing stage}, utilizing VLM and prior knowledge to simulate human understanding of video stories from three modalities. 
% 2) \textit{Multi-space chat stage}, creating growth-oriented characters through agent interactions in MAS, based on user queries and story stages. 
% 3) \textit{Scene customization stage}, expanding and visualizing various story scenes mentioned in the user dialogue.
% Applied to the Harry Potter series, our user study shows the system effectively portrays character emergent social behavior and growth, enhancing user interactive experience in the virtual story world.


Video story interaction enables viewers to engage with and explore narrative content for personalized experiences. 
However, existing methods are limited to user selection, specially designed narratives, and lack customization. 
To address this, we propose an interactive system based on user intent. Our system uses a Vision Language Model (VLM) to enable machines to understand video stories, combining Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and a Multi-Agent System (MAS) to create evolving characters and scene experiences.
It includes three stages: 1) Video story processing, utilizing VLM and prior knowledge to simulate human understanding of stories across three modalities. 2) Multi-space chat, creating growth-oriented characters through MAS interactions based on user queries and story stages. 3) Scene customization, expanding and visualizing various story scenes mentioned in dialogue.
Applied to the Harry Potter series, our study shows the system effectively portrays emergent character social behavior and growth, enhancing the interactive experience in the video story world.



\end{abstract}

