    All these intervals are disjoint; hence an optimal solution contains at least $k-1$ endpoints, i.e. at least $k-1$ guards. Thus the greedy sequence yields a solution of size at most $\opt+1$
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:repeatedgreedy_is_a_+1_approximation} is very powerful since we now only need to distinguish between the case where \RepeatedGreedy is optimal and the case where it uses one extra guard. We now consider how the greedy sequence behaves in different settings, first if $x_0$ is an endpoint of an optimal solution:

\begin{lemma}
