    Assume $g$ can see both edges connected to $c$. Since $c$ blocks $g$ from seeing any more than $z$, we know $z$, $g$ and $c$ are collinear. Let $a$ and $b$ be points on the edge after, respectively before $c$, which are visible from $c$. We now show, that either $a$ and $b$ lie on different sides of $\lineextension{g}{c}$, or one of the points lies on $\lineextension{g}{c}$.

