This can now be used to show where the blocking points are located in the figure:

\lemBlockingCornerNotInView*

\begin{proof}
    Assume for contradiction that $C \in [y, z]$. Since $c$ is a vertex of $P$, $c$ is neither $y$ nor $z$, thus $y$ and $z$ must lie on different sides of $c$ along $[y,z]$. From Lemma~\ref{lem:what_G_sees_around_C} we have two cases for what $g$ can see close to $c$ along $\partial P$.

    \begin{case}
        If $g$ cannot see both edges connected to $c$, either $[y,c]$ or $[c,z]$ is not visible to $g$ contradicting the fact that $g$ sees $[y,z]$.
    \end{case}

    \begin{case}
        If $g$ is able to see both edges connected to $c$, then we know one is contained in $\lineextension{g}{z}$ and the next edge is not visible from $g$, hence $z$ must lie on the edge connected to $c$, which is contained in $\lineextension{g}{z}$, for $[c,z]$ to be visible. However now $z$ can be moved until the endpoint of the edge containing $z$, hence $z$ is a vertex, which is contradicting the assumption (see Figure~\ref{fig:G_sees_more_than_just_z}).
        \popQED
    \end{case}
\end{proof}

