    We then consider the two functions $\Gsc_1$ and $\Gsc_2$.
    The first (see Figure~\ref{fig:two_greedy_functions} (left)) is defined as $\Gsc_1(x) = \{x + \frac1k - \frac\varepsilon k\}$ where $\varepsilon$ is some fixed small number and $\{a\}$ is the decimal part of $a$ (i.e. $a = \lfloor a \rfloor + \{ a \}$). This will require $k + 1$ guards, but $\varepsilon^{-1}$ steps of \Greedy will be required to satisfy one of the optimality conditions (in this case we satisfy Corollary~\ref{cor:escaping_an_interval_implies_optimal}).

