\subsection{Circular blast wave}

Next, we compute a circular shock wave emanating from the initial data found in \cite{Svard22}.  It is given by two states at $t=0$: "1" is inside a circle centred at $(x,y)=(0.5,0.5)$ with radius 0.25,  and "2" outside the circle.
\begin{align*}
\rho_1=1,\,\,p_1=1,\\
\rho_2=0.125,\,\,p_2=0.1.
\end{align*}
The velocity is initially zero for both states "1" and "2", and the gas constant is, $R=1/\gamma$. The boundary flux data vector is constructed using the state "2". We stress that the boundary data do not match the shock wave when it arrives at the boundary and hence there will be some reflections. 


