\subsubsection{Input Constraints}
Input constraint~\ref{opt1:con1}, $||x-x_{d}||_p\leq \epsilon$, defines a cone constraint.
% Given the overapproximation used to encode the dynamics function $f$, the resulting optimization problem is already mixed integer linear program (MILP)  without consideration of the input constraint.
If $p=1$ or $p=\infty$, the resulting expression may be encoded using linear constraints, e.g., 
$||x||_1 \leq t$ may be encoded $-z_i\leq x_i \leq z_i~,~\sum_iz_i\leq t$ and $||x||_{\infty} \leq t$ may be encoded $-t \leq x_i \leq t$.
If $p=2$ is chosen, the problem would become mixed integer convex, as the 2-norm is a convex function and $||x-x_{\text{data}}||_2\leq \epsilon$ would constrain the problem to sublevel sets of a convex function.
Note one is still able to obtain the global optimum for mixed integer convex problems (as for MILP).

