\section{Introduction}

High-dimensional partial differential equations (PDEs) arise in a wide range of applications, including finance, physics, and engineering. 
Traditional numerical methods for PDEs often suffer from the curse of dimensionality (CoD), where the computational cost increases exponentially with the dimensionality.  
Recent advances in deep learning have demonstrated significant potential for overcoming the CoD, yielding various deep learning-based numerical methods for high-dimensional PDEs.
Up to now, the most popular deep learning methods for solving PDEs can be broadly classified into two categories: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Direct approach}: neural networks are trained to  learn directly the solutions of the PDEs, as exemplified by the Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs) \cite{Raissi2019Physics,hu2024sdgd,Hu2024Hutchinson,He2023Learning,wang2022is,Gao2023Failure}, Deep Galerkin Method (DGM) \cite{Sirignano2018DGM,Al2022Extensions}, Deep Ritz \cite{E2018deep}, Weak Adversarial Networks (WANs) \cite{Zang2020Weak,Chen2023Friedrichs}, etc. 

    \item \textbf{SDE approach}: the PDE is reformulated into a stochastic differential equation (SDE) and then solved by deep learning. 
    Relevant works include DeepBSDE \cite{weinan2017deep,han2018solving,han2018convergence}, forward-backward stochastic neural networks~\cite{raissi2018forwardbackward,Zhang2022FBSDE}, deep splitting method \cite{beck2019deep}, deep backward schemes \cite{hure2020deep,germain2022Approximation},
    Actor-critic method \cite{Zhou2021Actor}, 
    deep neural networks algorithms \cite{Hure2021Deep,Bachouch2022Deep},
    diffusion loss \cite{Nusken2023Interpolating}, diffusion Monte Carlo-like approach \cite{Han2020Solving}, martginale neural networks \cite{cai2024socmartnet,cai2023deepmartnet,cai2024martnet}, etc.
\end{itemize}

To view the pros and cons of the two approaches above, we consider the quasilinear parabolic PDE as follows
\begin{equation}\label{eq_pde}
    \mathcal{D} v(t, x) = f(t, x, v(t, x)), \quad (t, x) \in [0, T] \times \R^d
\end{equation}
with a terminal condition $v(T, x) = g(x)$, $x \in \R^d$,
where $v: [0, T] \times \R^d \to \R$ is the unknown solution, and $\mathcal{D}$ is the nonlinear second-order differential operator defined as
\begin{equation}\label{eq_defD}
    \mathcal{D} := \partial_t + \mu^\top\br{t, x, v(t, x)} \partial_x + \frac{1}{2} \mathrm{Tr}\rbr{\sigma \sigma^{\top}(t, x, v(t, x)) \partial_{xx}^2}
\end{equation}
with $\mu$ and $\sigma$ are given functions valued in $\R^d$ and $\R^{d \times q}$, respectively, and $\mathrm{Tr}\rbr{\cdot}$ denotes the trace of a matrix.
The PDE \eqref{eq_pde} encompasses a wide range of important models, including the Allen-Cahn equation, Black-Scholes equation, Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman (HJB) equation, Kolmogorov backward equation, Burgers' equation, and various reaction-diffusion equations, etc.

When solving the PDE \eqref{eq_pde}, direct approaches typically train a neural network by minimizing the empirical loss function
\begin{equation}\label{eq_loss}
    L(v) := \frac{1}{M} \sum_{i=1}^M \abs{\mathcal{D} v(t_i, x_i) - f(t_i, x_i, v(t_i, x_i))}^2,
\end{equation}
where $(t_i, x_i)$ are sampled points in $[0, T] \times \R^d$.
A key advantage of \eqref{eq_loss} is that the computations for different sampled points $(t_i, x_i)$ are independent, making the method highly suitable for parallelization. 
However, these methods depend on automatic differentiation (AD) to evaluate the differential operator $\mathcal{D} v$. 
The back-propagation method to compute the $d \times d$ Hessian matrix is both computationally and memory intensive, especially in high dimensions, creating a bottleneck which has motivated a series of recent studies. 
For instance, \cite{He2023Learning} propose a Gaussian-smoothed neural network, enabling explicit computation of derivatives via Stein's identity. 
A series of recent works \cite{hu2024sdgd,Hu2024Hutchinson,shi2024stochastic} address this challenge by introducing techniques such as stochastic dimension gradient descent (SDGD), Hutchinson trace estimation, and stochastic Taylor derivative estimators in the framework of PINNs.

SDE-based approaches are grounded in the nonlinear Feynman-Kac formula, i.e., the solution $v$ to the PDE \eqref{eq_pde} can be represented as 
\begin{equation}\label{eq_feynman}
    Y_t = v(t, X_t), \quad Z_t = \partial_x v(t, X_t) \sigma(t, X_t, Y_t), \quad t \in [0, T],
\end{equation}
where $t \mapsto (X_t, Y_t, Z_t)$ are stochastic processes given by the forward-backward SDEs (FBSDEs):
\begin{equation}\label{eq_bsde}
\left\{\begin{aligned}
    X_t &= x_0 + \int_0^t \mu(s, X_s, Y_s) \di s + \int_0^t \sigma(s, X_s, Y_s) \di W_s,\\
    Y_t &= g(X_T) - \int_t^T f(s, X_s, Y_s) \di s - \int_t^T Z_s \di W_s,
\end{aligned}
\right.\quad t \in [0, T],
\end{equation}
where $\int_0^t \cdot \di W_s$ denotes the It\^o integral with respect to a standard Brownian motion $W_t$.
By reformulating the problem into the FBSDEs~\eqref{eq_bsde},
SDE-based approaches avoid the need to compute $\mathcal{D} v$ directly. 
However, this advantage comes at the cost of reduced parallelism in time compared to direct approaches. 
Specifically, 
(i) For quasilinear parabolic PDEs \eqref{eq_pde}, many methods such as \cite{weinan2017deep,han2018solving,han2018convergence,raissi2018forwardbackward,Zhang2022FBSDE,Nusken2023Interpolating,Han2020Solving,Zhou2021Actor} rely on simulating sample paths that depend on the unknown process $Y_t = v(t, X_t)$ being learned. 
Consequently, these sample paths need to be updated iteratively during training, and such updates are inherently sequential in time, resulting in increased computational cost.
(ii) The methods in  \cite{beck2019deep,hure2020deep,germain2022Approximation,Hure2021Deep,Bachouch2022Deep} propose to solve the problems backwardly in time, which can 
reduce training difficulty, but limits the time parallelizability of the algorithm as well.

To enhance the parallelizability of SDE-based approaches, the SOC-MartNet method \cite{cai2024socmartnet} and its derivative-free variant \cite{cai2024martnet} reformulate the PDE \eqref{eq_pde} as a martingale problem. 
This reformulation enables path sampling to be conducted offline, thereby allowing for highly parallelized online training. 
As a result, computational efficiency is significantly improved, making it possible to solve high-dimensional PDEs with up to $10^4$ dimensions.
However, the martingale deep learning methods proposed in \cite{cai2024socmartnet,cai2023deepmartnet,cai2024martnet} employ the tools of the It\^o calculus, which may create a challenge for those without a solid background in stochastic analysis. Thus, it is the intention of this paper to
% This work is a continuation of the martingale deep learning methods \cite{cai2024socmartnet,cai2023deepmartnet,cai2024martnet} for PDEs.
% Here we seeks to 
reinterpret the method proposed in \cite{cai2024socmartnet,cai2023deepmartnet,cai2024martnet}  from a new perspective similar to the direct approach framework, without using the knowledge of the stochastic calculus.
Moreover, it will be seen that the new approach will also provide a theoretical justification for the effectiveness of the proposed method, thus the SOC-MartNet method as well.
Specifically, we introduce a random difference method (RDM) for approximating the operator $\mathcal{D}$ in \eqref{eq_defD}.
The RDM only involves a first-order difference, thereby, avoiding explicit Hessian computation.
By integrating the RDM with the WAN approach \cite{Zang2020Weak}, we avoid the pointwise evaluation of expectations introduced by the RDM, resulting in a deep random difference method (DRDM) for solving the PDE \eqref{eq_pde}.
We further provide rigorous error estimates for the DRDM in the case where the differential operator $\mathcal{D}$ in \eqref{eq_defD} is linear.
Numerical experiments demonstrate that the proposed method can efficiently solve quasilinear parabolic PDEs and HJB equations in dimensions up to $10^4$ and $10^5$, respectively. 

The DRDM has several advantages.
First, although it is equivalent to the derivative-free martingale method in \cite{cai2024martnet}, its derivation does not rely on stochastic calculus; only Taylor expansions and basic properties of random variables are required.
Second, it combines the strengths of both direct and SDE-based approaches for PDEs: 
it avoids the use of AD for computing the derivatives in the PDEs and enables parallel computation of the loss function in both time and space.

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. 
\Cref{sec_meth} introduces the RDM for differential operators and its application to the PDE \eqref{eq_pde}. 
\Cref{sec_rdo_pde} presents the deep learning implementation of the DRDM for solving \eqref{eq_pde} and extends the approach to HJB equations. 
Numerical experiments are reported in \cref{sec_numexp}. 
Finally, concluding remarks are given in \cref{sec_conclusion}. 
The theoretical error analysis and the detailed parameter settings for the numerical experiments are provided in the Supplementary Materials.

\section{Random difference method}\label{sec_meth}

In this section, we present the random difference approximation to the differential operator defined in \eqref{eq_defD}. 
This technique forms a central component of our RDM  for solving PDEs.

\subsection{Basic expansion}\label{sec_basic}

We begin by considering the Laplace operator $\Delta = \sum_{i=1}^q \partial_{z_i}^2$ acting on a function $z \mapsto f(z)$, where $z \in \R^q$ and $f$ has bounded fourth-order derivatives.  
Let $\xi = \br{\xi_1, \xi_2, \cdots, \xi_q}^{\top}$ be a $\R^q$-valued random variable. 
By the Taylor expansion of $f(z)$ around $z = 0$, we obtain
\begin{equation}\label{eq_taylor}
    \E{f(\sqrt{h} \xi)} = f(0) + \sum_{k=1}^3 \frac{h^{k/2}}{k!}\E{\vbr{\big}{\xi^{\top} \partial_z}^k f(0)} + \frac{h^2}{4!}\E{\vbr{\big}{\xi^{\top} \partial_z}^4 f(c\sqrt{h} \xi)},
\end{equation}
where $\xi^{\top} \partial_z := \sum_{i=1}^q \xi_i \partial_{z_i}$, $\abs{\xi}^2 := \xi^{\top} \xi$, and $c \in [0, 1]$ depending on $\sqrt{h} \xi$.
Assume the components $\xi_1, \xi_2, \cdots, \xi_q$ of $\xi$ satisfy:
\begin{equation}\label{eq_condi_xi}
    \E{\xi_i} = 0, \quad \E{\xi_{ij}} = \delta_{ij}, \quad \E{\xi_i \xi_j \xi_k} = 0, \quad \E{\abs{\xi_i \xi_j \xi_k \xi_l}} < \infty
\end{equation}
for $i, j, k, l = 1, 2, \cdots, q$, 
where $\delta_{ij}$ denotes the Kronecker delta. 
Under these conditions, the first- and third-order derivative terms in \eqref{eq_taylor} vanish, yielding
\begin{equation}\label{eq_rdo_expa}
    \E{f(\sqrt{h} \xi)} = f(0) + \frac{h}{2} \sum_{i=1}^q \partial_{z_i}^2 f(0) + O\br{h^2}.
\end{equation}
The above expansion serves as the basis for the RDM to be introduced in the next subsection.

\begin{remark}
    Typical examples of $\xi$ that satisfy \eqref{eq_condi_xi} include: 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\xi \sim \mathrm{N}(0, I_q)$, where $I_q$ is the $q$-dimensional identity matrix;
        \item $\xi$ has mutually independent components, each satisfying
        \begin{equation}
            \Pra{\xi_i = c} = \Pra{\xi_i = -c} = \frac{1}{2 c^2}, \quad \Pra{\xi_i = 0} = 1 - \frac{1}{c^2}
        \end{equation}  
        for some constant $c \geq 1$.  
    \end{itemize}
\end{remark}

\subsection{RDM for convection-diffusion operator}\label{sec_condiff}

Next, we consider the convection-diffusion operator $\mathcal{D}$ as defined in \eqref{eq_defD}.
For a fixed point $(t, x) \in [0, T] \times \R^d$, define the function
\begin{equation}\label{eq_defVsz}
    V(s, z) := v(t + s, x + \mu s + \sigma z), \quad (s, z) \in [T - t] \times \R^q, 
\end{equation}
where, for clarity, we suppress the dependence of $V$, $\mu$, and $\sigma$ on $(t, x, v)$.
Applying the expansion \eqref{eq_rdo_expa} with $f$ replaced by $z \mapsto V(h, z)$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq_expandV}
    \E{V(h, \sqrt{h}\xi)} = V(h, 0) + \frac{h}{2} \sum_{i=1}^q \frac{\partial^2 V}{\partial z_i^2} (h, 0) + O\br{h^2}. 
\end{equation}
By the definition in \eqref{eq_defVsz}, the Taylor expansion and the chain rule imply
\begin{align}
    V(h, 0) &= V(0, 0) + \br{\partial_t v(t, x) + \mu^{\top} \partial_x v(t, x)} h + O(h^2), \label{eq1_expandV}\\
    \sum_{i=1}^q \frac{\partial^2 V}{\partial z_i^2} (h, 0) &= \sum_{i=1}^q \frac{\partial^2 V}{\partial z_i^2}(0, 0) + O(h) = \mathrm{Tr}\rbr{\sigma \sigma^{\top} \partial_{xx}^2 v(t, x)} + O(h). \label{eq2_expandV}
\end{align}
Inserting \eqref{eq1_expandV} and \eqref{eq2_expandV} into \eqref{eq_expandV}, we obtain 
\begin{equation}\label{eq_expandV2}
    \E{V(h, \sqrt{h}\xi)} = V(0, 0) + h \vbr{\big}{\partial_t + \mu^{\top} \partial_x + \frac{1}{2} \mathrm{Tr}\rbr{\sigma \sigma^{\top} \partial_{xx}^2}} v(t, x) + O(h^2). 
\end{equation}
By substituting \eqref{eq_defD} and \eqref{eq_defVsz} into the above equation, we arrive at the random difference approximation for $\mathcal{D}$ as 
\begin{equation}\label{eq_rdo}
    \mathcal{D}_h v(t, x) := \E{\frac{v(t + h, x + \mu h + \sqrt{h} \sigma \xi) - v(t, x)}{h}} = \mathcal{D}v(t, x) + O(h). 
\end{equation}
A rigorous derivation of \eqref{eq_rdo}, along with an explicit upper bound for the remainder term $O(h)$, is provided in the Supplementary Materials.

A notable feature of \eqref{eq_rdo} is that it enables the approximation of second-order derivatives using only first-order differences, thereby avoiding explicit computation of the Hessian.

\begin{remark}
    The approximation \eqref{eq_rdo} can also be derived from the It\^o formula under the regularity condition $v \in C^{1, 2}$; see, e.g., \cite[Theorem 2.3.3]{Zhang2017Backward}. A similar approach using random difference was considered in \cite{zhang2025shotgun}.
\end{remark}

