In this example, we examine how different spatial sampling strategies affect the performance of our method.
The benchmark problem is the convection-diffusion equation~\eqref{eq_linearPDE}, which features a  solution to highlight the importance of spatial sampling.
For clearer visualization, we set the spatial dimension $d = 1$ and consider two cases: $c = 1$ and $c = 5$ resulting different sharpness of the shock.
We compare two strategies for generating spatial sampling points at time $t_n$:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dynamics}: The spatial samples at $t = t_n$ are generated by simulating the paths $X_n^m$ according to \eqref{eq_pathX}, where the initial points $X_0^m$ are drawn uniformly from $[-1, 1]$. This strategy exploits the dynamics of the PDE~\eqref{eq_linearPDE} and is theoretically supported by our analysis in \Cref{sec_pathsamp}.

    \item \textbf{Plain}: The spatial samples at $t = t_n$ are given by $X_n^m = X_0^m + W_{t_n}^m$ for $m=1, 2, \cdots, M$, where $X_0^m$ and $W_{t_n}^m$ are i.i.d. samples from the uniform distribution on $[-1, 1]$ and the normal distribution $\mathrm{N}(0, t_n)$, respectively.
    This sample method serves as a baseline for comparison.
\end{itemize}

The corresponding numerical results are shown in \Cref{fig_linearPDE}.
We make the following observations:
\begin{itemize}
    \item In the first column of \Cref{fig_linearPDE}, the spatial samples generated by the dynamics strategy (first two rows) tend to avoid the shock region (i.e., the area near $x = 0$), especially for larger $c$ values. 
    In contrast, the plain strategy (last two rows) produces more samples in the shock region, since the Gaussian distribution of $W_{t_n}^m$ is centered at $x = 0$.
    Interestingly, as shown in the third column, the dynamics strategy yields better results than the plain strategy, even though the latter provides more samples in the shock region. 

    \item The observed phenomenon can be explained by the error propagation mechanism described in \eqref{eq_v0x0}. 
    The dynamics-based sampling strategy generates points according to the PDF $p(t, x)$ in \eqref{eq_v0x0}, which effectively targets regions where the residual has a significant impact on the error at $x \in [-1, 1]$, $t = 0$.
    In contrast, although the plain strategy places more samples in the shock region, the residual errors in that area have limited influence on the solution at $x \in [-1, 1]$, $t = 0$.

    \item The center column of \Cref{fig_linearPDE} shows the profile of the exact solution $v(t, x)$ at $t = 0$ along the sampling path $t \mapsto X_t$.
    Notably, the solution is significantly smoother along the path generated by the dynamics-based sampling strategy compared to the plain strategy.
    This observation aligns with the method of characteristics, 
    where solution information propagates along characteristic curves.
    Dynamics-based sampling may thus reduce the difficulty of function approximation in high-dimensional spaces, which is an interesting direction for future research.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}\label{sec_conclusion}

In this work, we proposed a deep random difference method (DRDM) for solving high-dimensional quasilinear parabolic PDEs and HJB equations. 
The DRDM approximates the convection-diffusion operator using only first-order differences, thereby avoiding explicit computation of Hessians and reducing reliance on automatic differentiation. By integrating DRDM within a Galerkin framework, we eliminate the need for pointwise evaluation of expectations, which enables efficient and highly parallelizable implementations. 
The method is also extended to HJB equations. 
We have provided rigorous error estimates for the linear case and demonstrated through numerical experiments that DRDM can efficiently and accurately solve PDEs in dimensions up to $10^5$.

The DRDM recovers existing martingale deep learning methods~\cite{cai2024socmartnet,cai2023deepmartnet,cai2024martnet} without relying on stochastic calculus. 
Our method offers several distinctive advantages:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The loss function does not require AD for any PDE derivatives, resulting in efficient computation and reduced memory usage.
    \item The loss function can be computed in parallel across both time and space, further enhancing efficiency.
    \item The sample distribution can be tailored to the dynamics of the convection-diffusion operator, enhancing the method's ability to handle problems with complex solution structures.
    \item The truncation error of the DRDM achieves first-order accuracy in time, which is higher than the half-order accuracy of methods based on the pathwise properties of the Euler-Maruyama scheme.
\end{itemize}
Future work will explore the application of DRDM to more challenging problems, including convection-diffusion equations with complex boundaries, committor functions, and non-convex SOCPs.


