\begin{abstract}
\emph{Suffixient sets} are a novel prefix array (PA) compression technique based on \emph{subsampling} PA (rather than compressing the entire array like previous techniques used to do): by storing very few entries of PA (in fact, a compressed number of entries), one can prove that pattern matching via binary search is still possible provided that random access is available on the text. In this paper, we tackle the problems of determining whether a given subset of text positions is (1) a suffixient set or (2) a suffixient set of minimum cardinality. We provide linear-time algorithms solving these problems. 


\keywords{Compressed text indexing \and suffixient sets \and suffix array}
\end{abstract}
%
%
%
\section{Introduction}

The ever-growing amount of repetitive data generated in bioinformatics sequencing projects \cite{1000genomes,100K,rice,arab} has motivated an increasing interest in developing new indexing methods capable of addressing the computational challenges posed by large-scale data processing. Notably, an increase in data size does not always correspond to a proportional increase in the informational content. This is particularly evident in genomic data, where genomes from individuals of the same species are often almost identical and therefore highly compressible. Compressed text indexes take advantage of this redundancy by supporting efficient pattern matching functionalities directly on the compressed data, without the need to decompress it \cite{Navarro21}.

The Suffix array (SA) \cite{ManberM93} is a classical, uncompressed solution to the indexed pattern matching problem. Pattern matching using SA can be efficiently performed through a simple binary search, combined with text extraction to compare the search pattern against the text’s suffixes during the search. Unfortunately, the SA scales linearly with the dataset size, making it unsuitable for handling large-scale, repetitive data, such as genomic sequence collections. Over the past few decades, many techniques have been proposed to encode the SA in a compact space \cite{GrossiV05,FerraginaM05,GagieNP20}. However, these solutions often introduce additional complexity, sacrificing the simplicity that made the original method so efficient.

Suffixient sets (see Definition \ref{def:suffixient} for a formal definition) are a new suffix array compression technique introduced by Depuydt et al.\ in 2024 \cite{Depuydt24,TOCSpaper}. Unlike previous approaches, this method compresses the Prefix Array (PA), i.e., the mirror version of the SA storing the co-lexicographic order of the text's prefixes, by subsampling a small set of positions corresponding to right-maximal substrings in the original text, rather than encoding the full PA. In \cite{TOCSpaper}, we proved that this subset of PA positions is sufficient to support pattern matching queries via binary search, just as with the original suffix array, when providing random access to the text. In particular, this novel compression scheme outperforms the $r$-index \cite{GagieNP20} both in time and space due to a better memory locality and to the fact that the size of the smallest suffixient set is often smaller than the number of equal-letter runs in the Burrows-Wheeler transform (the parameter that ultimately determines the size of the r-index). 

The size of indexes based on suffixient sets, such as the suffixient array in \cite{TOCSpaper}, is directly influenced by the cardinality of these sets. This underlines the importance of computing a suffixient set with the fewest amount of samples. In a previous work presented at SPIRE \cite{CenzatoOP24}, we addressed the problem of computing a suffixient set of minimum cardinality for any given text. 
That work, however, left open the following complementary (and natural) questions: 

\begin{problem}[Sufficiency]\label{problem1}
    Given set $S \subseteq [1,n]$ and a text $T \in \Sigma^n$, is $S$ a suffixient set for $T$?
\end{problem}


\begin{problem}[Minimum sufficiency]\label{problem2}
    Given set $S \subseteq [1,n]$ and a text $T \in \Sigma^n$, is $S$ a suffixient set of minimum cardinality for $T$?
\end{problem}

Observe that these natural problems were tackled for string attractors in \cite{kempa2018string}. Suffixient sets are indeed string attractors \cite{TOCSpaper}, but possess additional properties which make the algorithm in \cite{kempa2018string} not suitable for solving the above problems. 


In this paper, we show that Problems \ref{problem1} and \ref{problem2} can be solved in linear time. An implementation of our algorithms is publicly available online in the repository {\tt \url{https://github.com/regindex/suffixient}}.




\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:preliminaries}
Given $i, j, n \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $1, \leq i, j \leq n$, and given a finite alphabet $\Sigma = \{1, \dots, \sigma\}$, we use the following notation: $[i, j]$ defines an interval indicating all values $\{i, i + 1, \dots, j\}$, if $i \leq j$ and $[i, j] = \emptyset$, otherwise; $[n]$ for the interval $[1, n]$; $T = T[1, n]$ for a length-$n$ string $T$ over $\Sigma$; $\vert T \vert$ for the length of $T$; $\varepsilon$ for the empty string, which is the only string satisfying $\vert \varepsilon \vert = 0$; $T[i]$ for the $i$-th character of $T$; $T[i, j]$ for the substring $T[i] \dots T[j]$, if $i \leq j$, and $T[i, j] = \varepsilon$, otherwise; $T[1, i]$ for a prefix of $T$ ending a position $i$, and $T[j, n]$ for a suffix of $T$ starting at position $j$. We define $\rev{T} = T[n] \dots T[1]$. We assume $T$ begins with a special character $T[1] = \$$ which is smaller than any other character in $\Sigma$ and occurs only in $T[1]$, so $\rev{T}[n] = \$$. In addition, we assume $2 \leq n$ so $2 \leq \sigma$. We, moreover, assume that $\Sigma$ is an effective alphabet (namely, each character of $\Sigma$ appears in $T$), since any finite alphabet can be remapped to an effective alphabet without changing any of the complexities shown in this paper. 

Given an integer array $\mathcal{A}$ and $S \subseteq [\vert \mathcal{A} \vert]$, we use the notation $\mathcal{A}[S]$ to denote the set $\{\mathcal{A}[i] \mid i\in S\}$.
Moreover, we define $\argmin \mathcal{A}[S] = \{a \mid \mathcal{A}[a] = \min \mathcal{A}[S] \textrm{ and } a \in S \}.$ Furthermore, we define $\argmax \mathcal{A}[S] = \{b \mid \mathcal{A}[b] = \max \mathcal{A}[S] \textrm{ and } b \in S \}.$ In addition, the \textit{previous smaller value} array of $\mathcal{A}$ is an integer array of length $\vert \mathcal{A} \vert$ defined as $\PSV(\mathcal{A})[i] = \max(\{j \,|\, j < i, A[j] < A[i]\}\cup \{0\})$ for all $i\in [\vert \mathcal{A} \vert]$. The \textit{next smaller value} array of $\mathcal{A}$ is defined as $\NSV(\mathcal{A})[i] = \min(\{j \,|\, j > i,A[j] < A[i]\}\cup \{ \vert \mathcal{A} \vert \})$ for all $i\in [\vert \mathcal{A} \vert]$.

We assume the reader to be familiar with the concepts of suffix array ($\SA$), Burrows-Wheeler Transform ($\BWT$), and Longest Common Prefix array ($\LCP$). For space reasons, we give the formal definitions in Appendix~\ref{sec:deferred-preliminaries}. By $\BWTr$, $\SAr$ and $\LCPr$ we mean $\BWT(\rev{T})$, $\SA(\rev{T})$, and $\LCP(\rev{T})$, respectively. Given $T \in \Sigma^n$ and $i \in [n]$, by $text(i) = n - \SAr[i] + 1$ we indicate the position in $T$ corresponding to the character $\BWTr[i]$. Moreover, given $x \in [n]$ we denote by $bwt(x) = \SAr^{-1}[n + 1 - x]$ the position in $\BWTr$ of the character $T[x]$. For any $i \in [2, n]$ and $a, c \in \Sigma$, we say $i$ is a $c$-run break if $\BWTr[i- 1, i] = ac$ or $\BWTr[i - 1, i] = ca$ and $a \neq c$. We use $box(i)$ to denote the maximal interval $[j, k]$ such that $i \in [j, k]$ and $\LCPr[i] \leq \LCPr[h]$, for $h \in [j, k]$. We compute $box(i) = [l_i, r_i] = [\PSV(\LCPr)[i] + 1, \NSV(\LCPr)[i] - 1]$ in $O(1)$ time. For brevity, by $\PSV$ and $\NSV$ we mean $\PSV(\LCPr)$ and $\NSV(\LCPr)$, respectively.

For any two strings $\alpha, \beta \in \Sigma^*$, the longest common suffix between $\alpha$ and $\beta$ is defined as $lcs(\alpha, \beta) = \ell$ if and only if for every $i \in [\ell]$ it holds $\alpha[\vert \alpha \vert - i + 1] = \beta[\vert \beta \vert - i + 1]$ and $\ell = \min(\vert \alpha \vert, \vert \beta \vert)$ or $\alpha[\vert \alpha \vert - \ell] \neq \beta[\vert \beta \vert - \ell]$.

We say that $T[i, j]$ ($i - 1 \leq j$) is a right-maximal substring of $T \in \Sigma^n$ if $T[i, j]$ is a suffix of $T$ or there exists $a, b \in \Sigma$ such that $a \neq b$ and both $T[i, j]\cdot a$ and $T[i, j]\cdot b$ occur in $T$. Also, for $c \in \{a, b\}$ we say that $T[i, j]\cdot c$ is a one-character rigth-extension. In particular, we say that $T[i, j]\cdot c$ is a $c$-right-extension. Note that the empty string $\varepsilon$ is also right-maximal.


\section{Suffixient sets}
We start this section by recalling the suffixient set definition.

\begin{definition}[Suffixient set \cite{CenzatoOP24,Depuydt24}]\label{def:suffixient}
A set $S \subseteq [n]$ is  \emph{suffixient} for a string $T$ if, for every one-character right-extension $T[i,j]$ ($j\geq i$) of every right-maximal string $T[i,j-1]$, there exists $x\in S$ such that  $T[i,j]$ is a suffix of $T[1,x]$.
\end{definition}

Suffixient sets were initially proposed in \cite{Depuydt24}. In this paper, Depuydt et al. introduce a compressed text index that efficiently finds one occurrence of a given pattern, and that can be easily extended to find maximal exact matches. Their strategy relies on building a set $\SSS$ of size $O(\overline{r})$ that satisfies Definition~\ref{def:suffixient}, where $\overline{r}$ is the number of runs in $\BWTr$. Their index uses $O(\vert S \vert + g)$ words of space, where $g$ is the size of a grammar that represents the input text. We note that the smaller the suffixient set built, the smaller their index is.

Motivated by reducing as much as possible the space consumption of the above index, in \cite{CenzatoOP24}, Cenzato et al. studied methods to build suffixient sets of smallest cardinality. All of their algorithms that compute a smallest suffixient set rely on the notion of \textit{supermaximal extensions}.

\begin{definition}[Supermaximal extensions \cite{CenzatoOP24,Depuydt24}]\label{def:supermaximal-extensions}
We say that $T[i,j]$ (with $j \ge i$) is a \emph{supermaximal extension} if $T[i,j-1]$ is right-maximal, and for each right-maximal $T[i',j'-1] \neq T[i,j-1]$ (with $i' \le j' \le n$), $T[i,j]$ is not a suffix of $T[i',j']$.
\end{definition}

As proven in \cite{CenzatoOP24,Depuydt24}, the set described in the following definition is, indeed, a suffixient set of the smallest cardinality.

\begin{definition}[Suffixient set of smallest cardinality, \cite{CenzatoOP24,Depuydt24}]\label{def:smallest-suffixient}
Let $<_t$ be any total order on $[n]$. We define a set $\SSS \subseteq [n]$ as follows: $x \in \SSS$ if and only if there exists a supermaximal extension $T[i,j]$ such that (i) $T[i,j]$ is a suffix of $T[1,x]$, and (ii) for all prefixes $T[1,y]$ suffixed by $T[i,j]$, if $y\neq x$ then $y <_t x$.
\end{definition}

The measure $\chi$ (which is defined as the size of a smallest suffixient set) is also studied as a measure of repetitiveness, due to the combinatorial properties of suffixient sets.

\section{Testing suffixiency}\label{sec:testing-suffixiency}
In the search of a method to solve Problem~\ref{problem1}, as a first step, by Definition~\ref{def:suffixient} it seems natural to find a strategy to capture all of the rigth-maximal substrings of $T$. In that sense, the following observation will be useful.

\begin{observation}\label{obs:run-break-right-ext}
For each $c$-right-extension $T[k, l]$ $(k \leq l)$ of every right-maximal string $T[k, l - 1]$, there exists at least one $c$-run break $i$ such that $T[k, l]$ is a suffix of $T[text(i') - \LCPr[i], text(i')]$, with $i' \in \{i - 1, i\}$ and $\BWTr[i'] = c$.
\end{observation}
This observation induces an operative method to solve Problem~\ref{problem1}: for each run-break $i$, with $i' \in \{i - 1, i\}$ evaluate if there exists $x \in \SSS$ such that $T[text(i') - \LCPr[i], text(i')]$ suffixes $T[1, x]$.

In the following, we will show a method to compare those strings in $O(1)$ time which, at the end, leads to a linear-time algorithm. As a first step, we introduce the \textit{"SA leftmost position such that $T[1, x]$ is a right-extension"} array $\SLPR$ (Section~\ref{sec:slpr}). This array allows us to determine if one string suffixes another in $O(1)$ time, so we can easily develop a quadratic algorithm solving Problem~\ref{problem1} (Section~\ref{sec:quadratic}), and in Section~\ref{sec:linear} we present the linear-time version of this method. Finally, in Section~\ref{sec:cardinality} we present a method to solve Problem~\ref{problem2} by generalizing the approach used in the previous sections.

\subsection{The $\SLPR$ array}\label{sec:slpr}
Intuitively, given $x \in [n]$ and $i = bwt(x)$, $\SLPR[i]$ is a position in the $\LCPr$ array (not necessarily unique) such that $T[x - \LCPr[\SLPR[i]], x]$ is the larger suffix of $T[1, x]$ which is a right-extension. To compute this value, we start by identifying the closest run-breaks ($i - a$ to the left of $i$ and $bwt(i + b + 1)$ to the right) to each position $i$ of the $\BWTr$ (Definitions~\ref{def:a-below} and \ref{def:b-above}), we continue by computing the longest common suffix ($lcs$) between $T[1, x]$ and the strings induced by $(i - a)$ and $(i + b + 1)$ in $T$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:rm-from-left}), and finally showing that $\SLPR$ can be found by keeping the maximum between the sizes of those $lcs$ values (Corrollary~\ref{corollary:slpr}).

Let us start by identifying the "closests" run-break to each position in $\BWTr$.

\begin{definition}\label{def:a-below}
Let $x \in [n]$ and let $i = bwt(x)$. We say that $x$ is $a$-below if for some $a \in [0, i - 2]$ it holds $\BWTr[i - a - 1] \neq \BWTr[i - a] = \dots = \BWTr[i]$.
\end{definition}


\begin{definition}\label{def:b-above}
Let $x \in [n]$ and let $i = bwt(x)$. We say that $x$ is $b$-above if for some $b \in [0, n - i - 1]$ it holds $\BWTr[i] = \dots = \BWTr[i + b] \neq \BWTr[i + b + 1].$
\end{definition}


If $x \in [n]$ is $a$-below (resp. $b$-above), the following lemma establishes that there exists a position $k \in [i - a, i]$ (resp. $l \in [i + 1, i + b + 1]$) such that the string $T[text(k) - \LCPr[k], text(k)]$ (resp. $T[text(l - 1) - \LCPr[l], text(l - 1)]$) is equal to the longest common suffix between $T[1, x]$ and the right-extension $T[text(i - a) - \LCP[i - a], text(i - 1)]$ (resp. $T[text(i + b) - \LCP[i + b + 1], text(i + b)]$) induced by the closest run-break $(i - a)$ (resp. $(i + b)$) to the left of $i$ (resp. to the right of $i$).

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:rm-from-left}
Let $x \in [n]$ be $a$-below and let $i = bwt(x)$. For any $k \in \argmin\LCPr[i - a, i]$, we have
$$ lcs(T[1, x], T[text(i - a) - \LCPr[i - a], text(i - a)]) = T[text(k) - \LCPr[k], text(k)].$$ Moreover, let $y \in [n]$ be $b$-above, $j = bwt(y)$ and $Q = T[text(j + b) - \LCPr[j + b + 1], text(j + b)]$. For any $l \in \argmin\LCPr[j + 1, j + b + 1]$, we have
$$ lcs(T[1, y], Q) = T[text(l - 1) - \LCPr[l], text(l - 1)].$$
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
We show the proof for the $a$-below case. The proof for the $b$-above case is analogous.

We proceed by induction on $i - a$. For the base case, $a = 0$ and $i - a = i$, we have $\argmin\LCPr[i - a, i] = \{i\}$, so $k = i$, then $$ lcs(T[1, x], T[text(i) - \LCPr[i], text(i)]) = T[text(k) - \LCPr[k], text(k)], $$ and the claim holds. For the inductive step, assume that the claim holds for $(i - 1) - (a - 1)$, so $a > 0$ and $text(i - 1)$ is $(a - 1)$-below. Let $Q = T[text(i - a) - \LCPr[i - a], text(i - a)]$, $j \in \argmin\LCPr[i - a, i - 1]$, $l \in \argmin\LCPr[\{j, i\}] = \argmin\LCPr[i - a, i]$, and $P = T[text(j) - \LCPr[j], text(j)]$. Then, by induction hypothesis we have $lcs(T[1, text(i - 1)], Q) = P$.
By definition of $\LCPr$, it must hold $lcs(T[1, x], Q) = lcs(T[1, text(i - 1)], Q) = P$ or $lcs(T[1, x], Q) = lcs(T[1, x], T[1, text(i - 1)]) = T[x - \LCPr[i], x]$, so $lcs(T[1, x], Q) = T[x - \LCPr[l], x]$. Finally, by the minimality of $l$ we have $T[x - \LCPr[l], x] = T[text(l) - \LCPr[l], text(l)]$, as claimed.\qed
\end{proof}

If $x \in [n]$ is $a$-below, then $\min\LCPr[1, i] \leq \min\LCPr[i - a, i]$ always holds, so from Lemma~\ref{lem:rm-from-left} we can formulate the following Observation.

\begin{observation}\label{obs:right-max-from-left}
Let $x \in [n]$ be $a$-below, $T[x] = c$, and $i = bwt(x)$. For every $c$-run break $j < i - a$, such that $j' \in \{j - 1, j\}$ and $\BWTr[j'] = c$, it holds $\vert lcs(T[text(j') - \LCPr[j], text(j')], T[1, x]) \vert \leq \vert lcs(T[1, x], T[text(i - a) - \LCPr[i - a], text(i - a)]) \vert$.
\end{observation}

In other words, if $x \in [n]$ is $a$-below, there is no a $c$-run break $j < i - a$ such that $T[text(j') - \LCPr[j], text(j')]$ shares a suffix longer with $T[1, x]$ than $T[text(i - a) - \LCPr[i - a], text(i - a)]$ does.


Analogously, if $x \in [n]$ is $b$-above, then $\min\LCPr[i + 1, n] \leq \min\LCPr[i + 1, i + b + 1]$ always holds, so from Lemma~\ref{lem:rm-from-left} we can formulate the following Observation.

\begin{observation}\label{obs:right-max-from-right}
Let $x \in [n]$ be $b$-above, $T[x] = c$, and $i = bwt(x)$. For every $c$-run break $i + b + 1 < j$, such that $j'\in\{j - 1, j\}$ and $\BWTr[j'] = c$, it holds $\vert lcs(T[text(j') - \LCPr[j], text(j')], T[1, x]) \vert \leq \vert lcs(T[1, x], T[text(i + b) - \LCPr[i + b + 1], text(i + b)])$.
\end{observation}

In other words, if $x \in [n]$ is $b$-above, there is no $c$-run break $i + b + 1 < j$ such that $T[text(j') - \LCPr[j], text(j')]$ shares a suffix longer with $T[1, x]$ than $T[text(i + b) - \LCPr[i + b + 1], text(i + b)]$ does.

From Observations~\ref{obs:right-max-from-left} and \ref{obs:right-max-from-right}, given $x \in [n]$ and $i = bwt(x)$, we can note that $\SLPR[i] \in \argmin\LCPr[i - a, i]$ (if $x$ is only $a$-below) or $\SLPR[i] \in \argmin\LCPr[i + 1, i + b + 1]$ (if $x$ is only $b$-above) or $\SLPR[i]$ lies in the set with the maximum $\LCPr$ value (if $x$ is both). So, if for each of these sets we choose any position and keep the value having larger $\LCPr$ value, we have already computed $\SLPR[i]$. We arbitrarily chose the larger position from each of those sets.

Following the above idea, we define two arrays that capture those positions.

\begin{definition}[$\SBPR$ and $\SAPR$ arrays]
Let $x \in [n]$ and $i = bwt(x)$. We define the array $\SBPR[1, n]$ as follows: if $x$ is $a$-below for some $a \in [0, i - 2]$, then $\SBPR[i] = \max\argmin\LCPr[i - a, i]$, otherwise, $\SBPR[i] = 1$. Similarly, we define the array $\SAPR[1, n]$ as follows: if $x$ is $b$-above for some $b \in [0, n - i - 1]$, then $\SAPR[i] = \max\argmin\LCPr[i + 1, i + b + 1],$ otherwise, $\SAPR[i] = 1$.
\end{definition}

From Lemma~\ref{lem:rm-from-left} and Observations~\ref{obs:right-max-from-left} and \ref{obs:right-max-from-right}, we obtain the following Corollary.

\begin{corollary}\label{corollary:slpr}
Let $x \in [n]$, $i = bwt(x)$ and $$j = \max\argmax\LCPr[\{\SBPR[i], \SAPR[i]\}].$$ We have $T[x - \LCPr[j], x]$ is a right-extension and $x - \LCPr[j] = 1$ or $T[x - \LCPr[j] - 1, x]$ is not a right-extension. Moreover, it holds $T[x - \LCPr[j], x] = T[text(j - d) - \LCPr[j], text(j - d)]$, with $d = 0$ if $j \leq i$, and $d = 1$, otherwise.
\end{corollary}

From the result of the above corollary, we obtain the following.

\begin{definition}[$\SLPR$ array]
We define the array $\SLPR[1, n]$ as follows: for every $x \in [n]$, $\SLPR[bwt(x)] = \max\argmax\LCPr[\{\SBPR[bwt(x)], \SAPR[bwt(x)]\}].$ 
\end{definition}


It is easy to see that $\SLPR$ array can be computed in $O(n)$ time by one scan of the $\BWTr$ from $2$ to $n$ (to compute $\SAPR$ array), one scan from $n - 1$ to $1$ (to compute $\SBPR$ array), and a final scan from $1$ to $n$ (to compute the maximum between them). We summarize this procedure in Algorithm~\ref{alg:slpr} and provide a practical example in Figure~\ref{fig:slpr-example}.

\begin{figure}[tb]
\centering
\includegraphics[scale=0.65]{figures/running-example-slpr.pdf}
\caption{The figure shows the data used by Algorithm \ref{alg:slpr} to compute the $\SLPR$ array. For brevity, we show how Algorithm \ref{alg:slpr} works only on the position 16 (highlited in red). Since $\BWT[11, 20] = TAAAAAAAAG$, we have $text(16)$ is $4$-below and $3$-above. So, $\SBPR[16] = \max\argmin\LCP[\{12, 16\}] = \max\{14, 16\} = 16$ (highlighted in blue) and $\SAPR[16] = \max\argmin\LCP[\{17, 20\}] = \max\{19, 20\} = 20$ (highlighted in magenta). Finally, we have $\SLPR[16] = \max\argmax\LCP[\{16, 20\}] = 20$.
}\label{fig:slpr-example}
\end{figure}

\begin{algorithm}[tb]
\caption{Computing the $\SLPR$ array}\label{alg:slpr}
\Input{The $\LCPr$ and $\BWTr$ arrays for $T^{rev}$, with $T[1,n] \in \Sigma^n$}
\Output{The array $\SLPR$}
$\SAPR \gets [1 \times n];$ $\SBPR \gets [1 \times n];$ $\SLPR \gets [1 \times n]$\;
\For{$i = 2, \dots, n$}{
\If{$\BWTr[i - 1] \neq \BWTr[i]$}{
$\SBPR[i] \gets \LCPr[i]$\;
}
\Else{
$\SBPR[i] \gets \max(\argmin \LCPr[\{\SBPR[i - 1], i \}])$
}}
\For{$j = n - 1, \dots , 1$}{
\If{$\BWTr[j] \neq \BWTr[j + 1]$}{
$\SAPR[j] \gets j + 1$\;
}
\Else{
$\SAPR[j] \gets \max (\argmin \LCPr[\{j + 1, \SAPR[j + 1]\}])$\;
}}
\For{$i = 1, \dots , n$}{
$\SLPR[i] \gets \max(\argmax\LCPr[\{\SBPR[i], \SAPR[i]\}])$\;
}
\Return $\SLPR$\;
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{A simple quadratic algorithm}\label{sec:quadratic}

Having computed $\SLPR$, from the following lemma we obtain a method to evaluate if $T[1, x]$ is suffixed by a right-extension $T[i, j]$ in $O(1)$ time. 

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:rm-suffixes-slpr}
Given $x \in [n]$, let $i = bwt(x)$, and let $j$ be a $c$-run break, such that $j' \in \{j - 1, j\}$ and $\BWTr[j'] = c$. We have, $P = T[text(j') - \LCPr[j], text(j')]$ suffixes $T[1, x]$ if and only if $\BWTr[i] = c$ and $\PSV[j] < \SLPR[i] < \NSV[j]$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $d = 0$ if $j \leq i$ and $d = 1$ otherwise, and let $Q = T[text(\SLPR[i] - d) - \LCPr[\SLPR[i]], text(\SLPR[i] - d)]$.

\noindent($\Rightarrow$) Assume $P$ suffixes $T[1, x]$. Since by Observation~\ref{obs:run-break-right-ext} $P$ is a maximal extension, by definition of $\SLPR[i]$, $P$ also suffixes $Q$, thus we have $\SLPR[i] \in box(j)$, which implies $\PSV[j] < \SLPR[i] < \NSV[j]$. In addition, since $P$ suffixes $T[1, x]$, we have $T[text(j')] = T[x] = \BWTr[j'] = \BWTr[i] = c$.

\noindent($\Leftarrow$) We proceed by the contrapositive. Assume $P$ does not suffixes $T[1, x]$. Since $P$ is a maximal-extension and since $T[x - \LCPr[\SLPR[i]], x]$ is the largest maximal extension ending at position $x$, we have $T[text(j')] = \BWTr[j'] \neq \BWTr[i] = T[x]$ or $\vert lcs(P, T[1, x]) \vert < \LCPr[\SLPR[i]]$. Since by Lemma~\ref{lem:rm-from-left} we have $$T[x - \LCPr[\SLPR[i], x]] = T[text(\SLPR[i] - d) - \LCPr[\SLPR[i]], text(\SLPR[i] - d)],$$ then $\SLPR[i] \not\in box(j)$, so $\SLPR[i] \leq \PSV[j]$ or $\NSV[j] \leq \SLPR[i]$. \qed
\end{proof}

In other words, from Lemma~\ref{lem:rm-suffixes-slpr} we can note that if we want to know if $T[1, x]$ is suffixed by a right-maximal string $T[i, j]$ it would be enough to determine if $\SLPR[bwt(x)] \in box(bwt(j))$ and $\BWT[bwt(j)] = \BWT[bwt(x)]$.

From Observation~\ref{obs:run-break-right-ext} and Lemma~\ref{lem:rm-suffixes-slpr} we immediately obtain a simple algorithm to solve Problem~\ref{problem1}: for each $c$-run break $i$ in $\BWTr$ evaluate if there exists $x \in \SSS$ such that $\SLPR[bwt(x)] \in box(i)$ and $\BWTr[bwt(x)] = c$. Since there are $O(n)$ run-breaks in $\BWTr$, and in the worst case we have to scan the whole set $\SSS$ to find such an $x$, and $\vert \SSS \vert = O(n)$, this simple algorithm runs in $O(n^2)$ time.

\subsection{A linear time algorithm}\label{sec:linear}

The major drawback of the above strategy is that, for each run-break in $\BWTr$, in the worst case, we need to scan the whole set $\SSS$ we are evaluating for suffixiency. In the following, we propose a method to evaluate each position $x$ in $\SSS$ only while $T[1, x]$ is suffixed by the strings induced by the run-breaks we process, ultimately leading to a linear-time algorithm.

In a first step, we take advantage of the following.
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:not-in-box}
Let $i \in [n]$ and let $i \neq j \in [n]$ such that $j \not\in box(i)$. If $j < i$ then for any $k < j$, we have $k \not\in box(i)$. On the other hand, if $i < j$, then for any $j < g$, it holds $g \not \in box(i)$. 
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Since $j \not\in box(i)$, we have $j \leq \PSV[i]$ or $\NSV[i] \leq j$. In the former case, we have $j < i$, so $ k < j \leq \NSV[i]$ holds for any $k < j$, then $k \not\in box(i)$.
In the second case, we have $i < j$, thus $\NSV[i] < j < g$ holds for any $j < g$, so $g \not\in box(i)$.\qed
\end{proof}

For a given set $\SSS \subseteq [n]$, suppose that for some $c$-run break $i$ (with $i' \in \{i - 1, i\}$ and $\BWTr[i'] = c$) we want to determine if there exists $x \in \SSS$ such that $P = T[text(i') - \LCPr[i], text(i')]$ suffixes $T[1, x]$. Lemma~\ref{lem:not-in-box} tells us that if $\SLPR[bwt(x)] \not\in box(i)$, we have two cases: (i) if $\SLPR[bwt(x)] < i$, then for any $y \in \SSS$ such that $\SLPR[bwt(y)] < \SLPR[bwt(x)]$ we have $P$ does not suffix $T[1, y]$; otherwise, (ii) for any $z \in \SSS$ such that $\SLPR[bwt(x)] < \SLPR[bwt(z)]$ we have $P$ does not suffixes $T[1, z]$.

Following the above idea, we propose the first linear time algorithm to solve Problem \ref{problem1}. This procedure is summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:linear}.

The algorithm maintains three sets during the computation: a set $\mathcal{A} = \{bwt(x) \mid x \in \SSS\}$ (i.e., the elements of $\SSS$ replaced by their position in $\BWTr$), a set $\mathcal{B} = sort(\mathcal{A})$, and a set $\mathcal{C}$ such that $\mathcal{C}[c][k]$ is the position $h$ in $\mathcal{B}$ such that $\BWTr[\mathcal{B}[h]] = c$ is its $k$-th occurrence of $c$ (line 2). Now, for a given $c$-run break $i$, suppose that we scan $\mathcal{C}[c]$ from $1$ to $a \in [n]$ until we find for the first entry such that $\SLPR[\mathcal{C}[c][a]] \in box(i)$, then for the next $c$-run break $j$ (with $j' \in \{j - 1, j\}$ and $\BWT[j'] = c$) there is no need to scan positions $1, \dots, a - 1$ of $\mathcal{C}[c]$, since we know that $T[text(j') - \LCPr[j], text(j')]$ cannot suffix any of the prefixes of $T$ induced by those positions. Using this observation, we keep, for each $c \in \Sigma$, a pointer in $\mathcal{P}[c]$, pointing to the last visited element in $\mathcal{C}[c]$. Then, each time we find a $c$-run break $i$, we start comparing from the last pointed position of $\mathcal{C}[c]$. If we reach a position $h > i$, and $\SLPR[h] \not\in box(i)$, then $\SSS$ cannot be suffixient. 

Algorithm~\ref{alg:linear} runs in linear time and space. Computing arrays on line~\ref{line:linear_computing-arrays} takes $O(n)$ time. In addition, computing the sets $\mathcal{A}$ (by computing $\SAr^{-1}[n - s + 1]$ in $O(1)$ time for each $s \in \SSS$), $\mathcal{B}$ (by radix-sorting $\mathcal{A}$), and $\mathcal{C}$ (by traversing $\mathcal{B}$ and appending $\SLPR[i]$ into $\mathcal{C}[\BWTr(i)]$ in $O(1)$ for each $i \in \mathcal{B}$ using linked lists) on line~\ref{line:linear_computing-sets}  also takes $O(n)$ time. Finally, the rest of the algorithm is a scan of $\BWTr$, which can be done in $O(n)$ time, and a while loop inside that scan of $\BWTr$ which sequentially traverses in total at most the $\vert \SSS \vert$ positions in all the list in $\mathcal{C}$, so Algorithm~\ref{alg:linear} runs in linear-time.
Correctness follows from Lemmas~\ref{lem:rm-suffixes-slpr} and \ref{lem:not-in-box}.

In other words, we have proven the following.

\begin{lemma}
Given $T\in \Sigma^n$ and $\SSS \subseteq [n]$, Algorithm~\ref{alg:linear} solves Problem~\ref{problem1} in $O(n)$ time and $O(n)$ words of space.
\end{lemma}

In Figure~\ref{fig:test-example} we show an example of how Algorithm~\ref{alg:linear} works.

\begin{algorithm}[tb]
\caption{Determines if $\SSS$ is a suffixient set for a string $T\in\Sigma^n$}\label{alg:linear}
\Input{A text $T[1,n] \in \Sigma^n$ and $\SSS \subseteq [n]$}
\Output{$true$ if $\SSS$ is suffixient for $T$, $false$ otherwise}
{$\BWTr \gets \BWT(\rev{T})$;
$\LCPr \gets \LCP(\rev{T})$;
$\SAr^{-1} \gets \SA^{-1}(\rev{T})$;
$\PSV \gets \PSV(\LCPr)$;
$\NSV \gets \NSV(\LCPr)$;
$\SLPR \gets \SLPR(\LCPr, \BWTr)$\;}\label{line:linear_computing-arrays}
{$\mathcal{A} \gets \{bwt(s) = \SAr^{-1}[n - s + 1] \mid s \in \SSS\}$;
$\mathcal{B}[1, \vert A \vert] \gets sort(\mathcal{A})$;
$\mathcal{C}[1, \sigma] \gets ([~], \dots , [~])$;
$\mathcal{P}[1, \sigma] \gets (1, \dots, 1)$\;}\label{line:linear_computing-sets}
\textbf{for $k = 1, \dots, \vert\mathcal{B}\vert$ do}  $\mathcal{C}[\BWT[\mathcal{B}[k]]].append(\SLPR[\mathcal{B}[k]])$\;
\For{$i = 2, \dots, n$}{
\If{$\BWTr[i-1] \neq \BWTr[i]$}{
\For{$i' \in \{i - 1, i \}$}{
$c \gets \BWTr[i']$\;
\textbf{if $\vert \mathcal{C}[c] \vert = 0$ then} \textbf{return} $false$\;
\While{$\mathcal{C}[c][\mathcal{P}[c]] \leq \PSV[i]$}{
\textbf{if $\vert \mathcal{C}[c] \vert = \mathcal{P}[c]$ then} \textbf{return} $false$\;
$\mathcal{P}[c] \gets \mathcal{P}[c] + 1$\;
}
\textbf{if $\NSV[i] \leq \mathcal{C}[c][\mathcal{P}[c]]$ then return $false$}\;
}}}
\Return $true$\;
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Testing minimality}\label{sec:cardinality}

Let $\SSS \subseteq [n]$ be a suffixient set for $T \in \Sigma^n$. Suppose that there are two positions $x, y \in \SSS$, with $x \neq y$. If $T[x - \LCPr[\SLPR[bwt(x)], x]$ suffixes $T[y - \LCPr[\SLPR[y]], y]$, we know that $\SSS$ cannot be of smallest cardinality, since by keeping only $T[y - \LCPr[\SLPR[y]], y]$ we store the same information about right-maximal substrings of $T$ (in case of equality, breaking ties by $<_t$, according to Definition~\ref{def:smallest-suffixient}). In other words, we see that a suffixient $\SSS$ is of the smallest cardinality for $T$ if for each $x, y \in \SSS$ such that $x \neq y$ it holds $T[x - \LCPr[\SLPR[bwt(x)], x]$ does not suffix $T[y - \LCPr[\SLPR[y]], y]$. 

From the above analysis, we get the following.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:def-smallest-suffixient}
A suffixient set $\SSS$ is a suffixient set of the smallest cardinality for $T$ if for any $x, y \in \SSS$ such that $x \neq y$, it holds $\BWTr[bwt(x)] \neq \BWTr[bwt(y)]$ or $box(\SLPR[bwt(x)]) \cap box(\SLPR[bwt(y)]) = \emptyset$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $i = bwt(x)$ and $j = bwt(y)$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:rm-suffixes-slpr} we have that $P = T[x - \LCPr[\SLPR[i]], x]$ suffixes $Q = T[y - \LCPr[\SLPR[j]], y]$ if and only if $\BWTr[i] = \BWTr[j]$ and $\PSV[i] < \SLPR[j] < \NSV[i]$ which means $\SLPR[j] \in box(i)$. Similarly, we have that $Q$ suffixes $P$ if and only if $\BWTr[i] = \BWTr[j]$ and $\PSV[j] < \SLPR[i] < \NSV[j]$, which means $\SLPR[i] \in box(j)$. Then, if neither $P$ suffixes $Q$ nor $Q$ suffixes $P$ we have $\BWTr[i] \neq \BWTr[j]$ or $\SLPR[j] \not\in box(i)$ and $\SLPR[i] \not \in box(j)$, from which we conclude $box(\SLPR[bwt(x)]) \cap box(\SLPR[bwt(y)]) = \emptyset$, as claimed.
\qed
\end{proof}


We are now ready to show a linear time algorithm to solve Problem \ref{problem2}. This procedure is summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:cardinality}.

The algorithm computes the same set $\mathcal{C}$ as the one used in Algorithm~\ref{alg:linear}, and if for some $c \in \Sigma$ and $j \in [2, \vert \mathcal{C}[c] \vert]$ it holds $\PSV[\mathcal{C}[c][j]] < \NSV[\mathcal{C}[c][j - 1]]$ (so, their boxes overlap), we know that $\SSS$ cannot be a suffixient set of the smallest cardinality.
With respect to the space consumption, it takes $O(n)$ time to compute the same arrays as those used in Algorithm~\ref{alg:linear}. In addition, we sequentially scan the array $\mathcal{C}$, which has exactly $\vert \SSS \vert$ entries, so it takes $O(n)$ time.
Correctness follows from Lemma~\ref{lem:rm-suffixes-slpr} and \ref{lem:def-smallest-suffixient}.

In other words, we have proven the following.

\begin{lemma}
Given $T\in \Sigma^n$ and $\SSS \subseteq [n]$, Algorithm~\ref{alg:cardinality} solves Problem~\ref{problem2} in $O(n)$ time and $O(n)$ words of space.
\end{lemma}

In Figure~\ref{fig:test-example}, we show an example of how Algorithm~\ref{alg:linear} works.

\begin{figure}[!htb]
\centering
\includegraphics[scale=0.7]{figures/running-example-linear-test.pdf}
\caption{
