\begin{algorithm}[!htb]
\caption{Determines if a suffixient set $\SSS$ is of the smallest cardinality}\label{alg:cardinality}
\Input{$T\in\Sigma^n$ and a suffixient set $\SSS \subseteq [n]$}
\Output{$true$ if $\SSS$ is a suffixient set of smallest cardinality, $false$ otherwise}
{$\BWTr \gets \BWT(\rev{T})$;
$\SAr^{-1} \gets \SA^{-1}(\rev{T})$;
$\LCPr \gets \LCP(\rev{T})$\;}
{$\PSV \gets \PSV(\LCPr)$;
$\NSV \gets \NSV(\LCPr)$;
$\SLPR \gets \SLPR(\LCPr, \BWTr)$\;}
{$\mathcal{A} \gets \{\SAr^{-1}[n - x + 1] \mid x \in \SSS\}$;
$\mathcal{B}[1, \vert A \vert] \gets sort(\mathcal{A})$;
$\mathcal{C}[1, \sigma] \gets ([~], \dots , [~])$\;}
\textbf{for $k = 1, \dots, \vert\mathcal{B}\vert$ do}  $\mathcal{C}[\BWT[\mathcal{B}[k]]].append(\SLPR[\mathcal{B}[k]])$\;
\For{$i = 1, \dots, \sigma$}{
\For{$j = 2, \dots, \vert \mathcal{C}[i] \vert$}{
\textbf{if $\PSV[\mathcal{C}[i][j]] < \NSV[\mathcal{C}[i][j - 1]]$ then} \textbf{return} $false$\;
}}
\Return $true$\;
\end{algorithm}


\section{Conclusions and future work}

In this paper, we addressed two complementary and natural problems that were left open in our previous work in SPIRE \cite{CenzatoOP24}: determining 1) if a set $S \in [n]$ is suffixient for a text $T$ and if 2) $S$ is a suffixient set of minimum cardinality. We showed that both can be solved efficiently by extending the techniques developed in \cite{CenzatoOP24}. Specifically, we introduced two linear-time algorithms that solve these problems and provided a publicly available implementation of our solutions {\tt \url{https://github.com/regindex/suffixient}}.\\
%
\indent As future work, we plan to evaluate our algorithm's performance on real datasets, develop a compressed space version that can work for large-scale datasets, and explore potential applications in the text indexing domain.

