\subsection{Query Latency}

Figure~\ref{fig:ref8} shows the average query latency of different methods using the Llama3.1-8b model on TREC-DL 2019. We can obtain the following findings.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item RefRank and Pointwise methods have comparable query latencies, significantly lower than others. This can be attributed to two reasons: first, both the RefRank method and the Pointwise method can obtain document scores in a single inference, allowing for efficiency improvements through batching and parallel computation; second, the input lengths for the RefRank method and the Pointwise method are relatively short, leading to faster inference times.
    \item The query latency of the RefRank method is slightly higher than that of the Pointwise method, primarily due to the introduction of reference documents, which increases the input length.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Ablation Studies}

To systematically evaluate the robustness and effectiveness of the RefRank, we designed and conducted a series of ablation experiments to assess the performance of the framework from various perspectives.

\textbf{Robustness of Choosing Different Reference Documents.}
In our experiments, we selected the top-1 document for evaluation. Building on the analysis presented in Section 3.3, we computed the ranking results using the top-2 documents as reference documents. As illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:ref6}, the results on both the TREC-DL and BEIR datasets demonstrate stability, with an average fluctuation of merely 0.34\%.

\textbf{Robustness of Selecting Different Weighted numbers.}
In this experiment, we utilized the top-3, top-4, and top-5 weighted documents.  As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:ref7}, the results across the TREC-DL and BEIR datasets remain stable, with an average fluctuation of 0.66\%. Therefore, in practice, weighting the top-3 documents can better balance efficiency and effectiveness.

